// In-depth material (Science and Space, part 5): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
microbe:{s:[
["Where microbes live","Microbes live almost everywhere: in soil, water, air, ice, hot springs, deep rocks and inside other living things. Some, called extremophiles, thrive in boiling vents, salty lakes or acidic pools. A gram of soil can hold billions of bacteria. Microbes were the first life on Earth, appearing more than 3.5 billion years ago."],
["Bacteria up close","Bacteria are single cells without a nucleus, usually a few thousandths of a millimeter long. They come in shapes such as spheres, rods and spirals. Many have whip-like tails called flagella for swimming. Under good conditions, some can divide every 20 minutes, so one bacterium can become billions in a day."],
["Inside a virus","Viruses are much smaller than bacteria and are not cells. A virus is a strand of genetic material, DNA or RNA, inside a protein coat. It cannot grow or reproduce on its own; it must invade a living cell and hijack it to make copies. Viruses infect every kind of living thing, even bacteria."],
["Diseases","Some microbes cause disease. Bacteria cause tuberculosis, cholera, typhoid and strep throat. Viruses cause colds, flu, measles, dengue, rabies and COVID-19. Fungi cause athlete’s foot, and protozoa cause malaria. Clean water, hygiene, vaccines and medicines have greatly reduced deaths from infectious diseases."],
["Helpful microbes","Most microbes are harmless or helpful. Bacteria in soil recycle nutrients and help plants get nitrogen. Yeast makes bread rise. Bacteria turn milk into yogurt and cheese and help make vinegar, soy sauce and fish sauce. Around the world, microbes ferment foods such as Korean kimchi, German sauerkraut, Japanese miso and Ethiopian injera."],
["The microbiome","The human body hosts trillions of microbes, especially in the gut, together called the microbiome. They help digest food, make vitamins, train the immune system and protect against harmful germs. A varied diet rich in fiber supports a healthy microbiome, while overuse of antibiotics can disturb it."]],
sp:["Dengue, a spreading disease","Dengue is a viral disease spread by Aedes mosquitoes, which bite during the day and breed in clean, still water in containers, tires and flower pots. It causes high fever, severe headache, joint pain and sometimes dangerous bleeding. About half the world’s people now live where dengue can spread, and in 2024 more than 14 million cases were reported, a record. Removing standing water where mosquitoes breed is one of the best defenses."],
g:[["Microbe","A living thing too small to see without a microscope"],["Bacterium","A single-celled organism without a nucleus"],["Virus","A tiny particle that reproduces inside cells"],["Microbiome","The community of microbes living in a body"]],
f:["There are more bacterial cells in your body than there are stars in the Milky Way.","Nata de coco is made by bacteria fermenting coconut water.","The largest known virus, a giant virus, is bigger than some bacteria."]},
moon:{s:[
["The Moon’s orbit","The Moon orbits Earth about every 27.3 days at an average distance of about 384,400 km. Because Earth is also moving around the Sun, it takes about 29.5 days to go from one new moon to the next. The Moon’s orbit is slightly oval, so it sometimes appears larger, a supermoon."],
["One face toward us","The Moon always shows the same face to Earth because it spins once on its axis in the same time it takes to orbit Earth, a situation called tidal locking. The far side, often wrongly called the dark side, gets as much sunlight as the near side. It was first photographed by the Soviet Luna 3 in 1959."],
["Craters and maria","The Moon’s surface is covered with craters made by asteroid and comet impacts over billions of years. With no air or weather, craters stay sharp. The dark patches, called maria, Latin for seas, are plains of solidified lava, not water. Tycho, a bright crater with long rays, is easy to see with binoculars."],
["Apollo landings","Between 1969 and 1972, six Apollo missions landed 12 astronauts on the Moon. They collected 382 kg of rocks and soil, set up experiments and drove lunar rovers on the last three missions. Their footprints and flags remain. Apollo 17’s Eugene Cernan was the last person to walk on the Moon, in December 1972."],
["The Moon and culture","The Moon has inspired myths, calendars and festivals. Many calendars, including the Islamic and Chinese calendars, are based on the Moon’s phases. Ramadan begins and ends with the sighting of the new crescent Moon, and the Mid-Autumn Festival, celebrated by Chinese and Vietnamese communities around the world, honors the full moon with mooncakes and lanterns."],
["Returning to the Moon","NASA’s Artemis program aims to return astronauts to the Moon, including the first woman, and build a long-term presence near the south pole, where ice may provide water. In April 2026, Artemis II carried four astronauts, three Americans and a Canadian, around the Moon and back, the first crewed flight to the Moon since 1972. China has landed rovers on both sides of the Moon and brought back samples from the far side in 2024. India’s Chandrayaan-3 landed near the south pole in 2023."]],
sp:["Earthrise","On 24 December 1968, the Apollo 8 astronauts became the first humans to orbit the Moon. As their spacecraft came around from the far side, they saw Earth rising over the gray lunar horizon, a blue and white ball against black space. Astronaut William Anders grabbed a camera and took a photograph now called Earthrise. It showed how small and fragile Earth is and helped inspire the environmental movement."],
g:[["Phase","The changing shape of the lit part of the Moon"],["Mare","A dark lava plain on the Moon"],["Crater","A bowl-shaped hollow made by an impact"],["Tidal locking","When one body always shows the same face to another"]],
f:["The Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 cm a year.","A person would weigh about one sixth as much on the Moon.","The Moon has moonquakes, some lasting for many minutes."]},
mars:{s:[
["Why Mars is red","Mars looks red because its surface is covered with iron oxide, or rust, in its rocks and dust. Fine red dust also fills the thin atmosphere, giving the sky a butterscotch color by day. Sunsets on Mars, strangely, look blue, because the dust scatters blue light toward the direction of the Sun."],
["Olympus Mons and Valles Marineris","Olympus Mons is the largest volcano in the Solar System, about 22 km high and about 600 km across. It is a shield volcano that grew huge because Mars has no moving plates. Valles Marineris is a system of canyons about 4,000 km long and up to 7 km deep, dwarfing the Grand Canyon."],
["A thin, cold world","Mars’s atmosphere is about 100 times thinner than Earth’s and mostly carbon dioxide. Average temperatures are about −60°C, though summer days near the equator can reach about 20°C. The planet has polar ice caps of water ice and frozen carbon dioxide that grow and shrink with the seasons."],
["Missions to Mars","After early flybys, NASA’s Viking landers touched down in 1976 and searched for life. Rovers followed: Sojourner in 1997, Spirit and Opportunity in 2004, Curiosity in 2012 and Perseverance in 2021, which carried the Ingenuity helicopter, the first aircraft to fly on another planet. China’s Zhurong rover landed in 2021."],
["Looking for life","Scientists search for signs that Mars once supported life. Rovers have found ancient riverbeds, lake sediments and minerals that form in water, as well as organic molecules. Perseverance is collecting rock samples that future missions may bring back to Earth for study. So far, no definite evidence of life has been found."],
["Humans to Mars","Space agencies and companies hope to send people to Mars. The journey would take about seven months each way, and astronauts would face radiation, low gravity and isolation. They would need to make water, oxygen and fuel from Martian resources. Some plans aim for the 2030s or later."]],
sp:["Ingenuity","Ingenuity was a small helicopter, weighing just 1.8 kg, carried to Mars under the belly of the Perseverance rover. On 19 April 2021, it made the first powered, controlled flight on another planet, hovering a few meters above the ground. Because Mars’s air is so thin, its blades spun about 2,400 times a minute. Planned for five test flights, it flew 72 times over nearly three years before a damaged blade ended its mission in January 2024."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that explores a planet’s surface"],["Shield volcano","A broad volcano with gentle slopes"],["Ice cap","A covering of ice at a planet’s pole"],["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"]],
f:["A day on Mars, called a sol, lasts about 24 hours 39 minutes.","Mars has the largest dust storms in the Solar System, sometimes covering the whole planet.","Mars has two tiny moons, Phobos and Deimos."]},
exoplanet:{s:[
["The first discoveries","The first planets found outside the Solar System, in 1992, orbited a pulsar, a dead star. In 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz discovered 51 Pegasi b, the first planet orbiting a Sun-like star, a giant planet circling closer than Mercury does to the Sun. They won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2019."],
["The wobble method","A planet’s gravity makes its star wobble slightly. As the star moves toward and away from us, its light shifts slightly toward blue and then red. By measuring these tiny shifts, astronomers can detect planets and estimate their mass. This radial velocity method found many of the first exoplanets."],
["Kepler and TESS","NASA’s Kepler space telescope, launched in 2009, stared at about 150,000 stars, watching for tiny dips in brightness as planets passed in front of them. It found thousands of planets and showed that planets are common. TESS, launched in 2018, surveys nearby bright stars across the whole sky."],
["Kinds of exoplanets","Exoplanets come in great variety: hot Jupiters orbiting very close to their stars, super-Earths larger than Earth but smaller than Neptune, mini-Neptunes, lava worlds and planets orbiting two suns, like Tatooine in Star Wars. Some planets drift alone through space without any star."],
["Habitable worlds","Astronomers search for rocky planets in the habitable zone, where it is neither too hot nor too cold for liquid water. Proxima b, orbiting the nearest star to the Sun, lies in its star’s habitable zone. The TRAPPIST-1 system, about 40 light-years away, has seven Earth-sized planets, several in the habitable zone."],
["Searching for life","The James Webb Space Telescope can study the atmospheres of some exoplanets by examining starlight that passes through them. It has detected water vapor, carbon dioxide and other molecules. Scientists hope future telescopes will be able to detect gases, such as oxygen and methane together, that could signal life."]],
sp:["TRAPPIST-1","TRAPPIST-1 is a small, cool red dwarf star about 40 light-years away, only slightly bigger than Jupiter. In 2017 astronomers announced that it has seven rocky, Earth-sized planets, all orbiting closer to their star than Mercury is to the Sun. Because the star is so dim, several of the planets may be at temperatures where liquid water could exist. Its planets are so close together that from the surface of one, the others would appear larger than our Moon."],
g:[["Exoplanet","A planet orbiting a star other than the Sun"],["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist"],["Red dwarf","A small, cool, dim star"]],
f:["More than 5,000 exoplanets have been confirmed.","Some exoplanets are thought to rain glass or iron.","Most stars in our galaxy probably have planets."]},
comet:{s:[
["Dirty snowballs","A comet’s solid center, the nucleus, is a mixture of ice, dust and rock, usually a few kilometers across. Astronomer Fred Whipple described comets as dirty snowballs in 1950. As a comet nears the Sun, its ice turns to gas, creating a glowing cloud called a coma and long tails that can stretch millions of kilometers."],
["Where comets come from","Short-period comets, which return in less than 200 years, come from the Kuiper Belt beyond Neptune. Long-period comets come from the distant Oort Cloud, which may extend a light-year or more from the Sun. A passing star or the gravity of the galaxy can nudge comets toward the inner Solar System."],
["The asteroid belt","Asteroids are rocky or metallic bodies, mostly found in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter. The largest, Ceres, is about 940 km across and is classed as a dwarf planet. Others, such as Vesta, are large enough to have layers. Some asteroids have their own tiny moons."],
["Meteors and meteorites","When small pieces of rock or dust enter Earth’s atmosphere at high speed, they burn up, creating streaks of light called meteors or shooting stars. Pieces that survive and land are meteorites. Most meteorites come from asteroids, but a few come from the Moon and Mars, knocked off by impacts."],
["Visiting comets and asteroids","Spacecraft have visited many of these small bodies. The European Rosetta probe orbited comet 67P from 2014 to 2016 and dropped the Philae lander on it. Japan’s Hayabusa2 and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx collected samples from asteroids Ryugu and Bennu and returned them to Earth, finding water-bearing minerals and organic molecules."],
["Protecting Earth","Large asteroid impacts are rare but dangerous. Astronomers track near-Earth objects to spot any threats early. In 2022 NASA’s DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into the small asteroid Dimorphos and changed its orbit, the first test of a way to deflect an asteroid. No known asteroid poses a significant threat in the next century."]],
sp:["Halley’s Comet","Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years. In 1705 Edmond Halley realized that comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 were the same object, and he predicted its return in 1758. It appeared on schedule after his death, and it was named in his honor. Records of the comet go back over 2,000 years, and it appears in the Bayeux Tapestry of 1066. It last passed in 1986 and will return in 2061."],
g:[["Comet","An icy body that grows a tail near the Sun"],["Asteroid","A rocky body orbiting the Sun"],["Meteor","A streak of light from a rock burning in the atmosphere"],["Meteorite","A space rock that reaches the ground"]],
f:["Tens of metric tons of space dust fall on Earth every day.","The Perseid meteor shower peaks every August.","A meteorite crater in Arizona, Meteor Crater, is about 1.2 km wide."]},
bigbang:{s:[
["An expanding universe","In the 1920s astronomers found that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and the farther away they are, the faster they recede. This means the universe is expanding. If you run time backward, everything must once have been packed together in an extremely hot, dense state. This idea is the Big Bang theory."],
["Lemaître’s primeval atom","In 1927 Georges Lemaître, a Belgian priest and physicist, proposed that the universe began from a single primeval atom that expanded. Many scientists were skeptical. The name Big Bang was coined in 1949 by Fred Hoyle, who supported a rival steady-state theory in which the universe had no beginning."],
["The first minutes","In the first fraction of a second, the universe may have expanded incredibly fast in a process called inflation. Within the first few minutes, it cooled enough for protons and neutrons to combine into the nuclei of hydrogen, helium and a little lithium. The amounts predicted match those observed today."],
["The afterglow","About 380,000 years after the Big Bang, the universe cooled enough for atoms to form, and light could travel freely. That light, stretched into microwaves by cosmic expansion, still fills the sky as the cosmic microwave background. It was discovered by accident in 1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, who won the Nobel Prize."],
["Age of the universe","By measuring the cosmic microwave background and the expansion rate, scientists estimate the universe is about 13.8 billion years old. Satellites such as COBE, WMAP and Planck mapped tiny temperature differences in the background radiation, the seeds from which galaxies grew."],
["The future of the universe","In 1998 astronomers discovered that the expansion of the universe is speeding up, driven by a mysterious dark energy that makes up about 68% of the universe. If this continues, galaxies will drift ever farther apart, and the universe may grow cold and dark over trillions of years. Scientists are still trying to understand dark energy."]],
sp:["The cosmic microwave background","In 1964 and 1965 Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, working at Bell Labs in New Jersey, were testing a large horn-shaped antenna and kept hearing a faint hiss coming from every direction. They even cleaned out pigeon droppings, suspecting the birds. The hiss turned out to be the oldest light in the universe, left over from the Big Bang. Their discovery strongly supported the Big Bang theory and earned them the 1978 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
g:[["Big Bang","The theory that the universe began in a hot, dense state"],["Inflation","A very rapid expansion in the first instant"],["Dark energy","An unknown force speeding up the expansion"],["Redshift","Light stretched to longer wavelengths"]],
f:["Some of the static on old analog TVs came from the cosmic microwave background.","The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across.","Hydrogen and helium make up about 98% of the ordinary matter in the universe."]}
});

;
