// In-depth material (History of Science, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
scimeth:{s:[
["Observation","Science begins with careful observation: noticing something and asking why. Scientists use their senses and instruments such as microscopes, telescopes and thermometers to collect data. Good observations are recorded carefully and measured where possible, so that others can check them."],
["Ibn al-Haytham","The Arab scientist Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo around 1000 CE, is often called a pioneer of the scientific method. In his Book of Optics, he tested ideas about light and vision with experiments using dark rooms and pinholes, rather than relying only on the authority of ancient writers. He urged scholars to doubt and check everything."],
["Bacon and Descartes","In the early 1600s, the English thinker Francis Bacon argued that knowledge should be built from careful observations and experiments, reasoning step by step to general laws. The French philosopher René Descartes stressed doubt and logical reasoning. Together their ideas shaped the Scientific Revolution."],
["Variables and controls","In an experiment, scientists change one thing, the independent variable, and measure its effect on another, the dependent variable, while keeping everything else the same. A control group, which does not receive the change, gives a baseline for comparison. Repeating experiments makes results more reliable."],
["Bias and blind tests","People tend to see what they expect, so scientists use methods to reduce bias. In a blind test, participants do not know whether they are getting the real treatment or a placebo. In a double-blind test, neither the participants nor the researchers know. Large, random samples also help avoid misleading results."],
["Science in everyday life","The scientific method is not only for laboratories. People use it when they test which fertilizer grows better rice, which route to school is quicker, or why a phone battery drains. Asking questions, testing ideas and changing your mind when the evidence shows you were wrong are skills useful to everyone."]],
sp:["Semmelweis and handwashing","In the 1840s, Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that far more mothers died of childbed fever in a Vienna hospital ward run by doctors than in one run by midwives. Doctors often came straight from dissecting bodies. He made them wash their hands in chlorinated lime, and deaths dropped sharply. Many doctors rejected his idea, and he died in 1865, before germ theory proved him right."],
g:[["Hypothesis","A testable explanation or prediction"],["Variable","A factor that can change in an experiment"],["Control","A comparison group that is not changed"],["Placebo","A fake treatment used for comparison"]],
f:["The word scientist was coined in 1833 by William Whewell.","A result that cannot be repeated by others is treated with suspicion.","Galileo is often called the father of modern science."]},
copern:{s:[
["Ptolemy’s universe","For about 1,400 years, most scholars followed the model of Claudius Ptolemy, a Greek astronomer in Alexandria around 150 CE. He placed Earth at the center, with the Sun, Moon, planets and stars circling it. To explain why planets sometimes seem to move backward, he added small circles called epicycles."],
["An ancient idea","The idea of a Sun-centered universe was not entirely new. Around 270 BCE, the Greek astronomer Aristarchus of Samos suggested that Earth goes around the Sun, but his idea was not widely accepted. Copernicus knew of Aristarchus and mentioned him in an early draft of his work."],
["On the Revolutions","Nicolaus Copernicus, a Polish astronomer and church official, set out his Sun-centered system in De revolutionibus orbium coelestium, On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, published in 1543. Legend says he received a printed copy on the day he died. A preface added by another person presented the idea as merely a convenient calculation."],
["How it explained the sky","In the Copernican system, Earth spins once a day and orbits the Sun once a year. The backward loops of planets such as Mars are illusions caused by Earth overtaking them. The system also explained why Mercury and Venus always appear close to the Sun. However, Copernicus still used perfect circles."],
["Bruno and the infinite universe","The Italian philosopher Giordano Bruno went further than Copernicus, suggesting the universe is infinite and that stars are distant suns with their own planets. He was burned at the stake in Rome in 1600, mainly for religious ideas considered heretical. Today he is remembered as a symbol of free thought."],
["Newton completes the picture","In 1687 Isaac Newton showed that the same force of gravity that makes an apple fall keeps the Moon and planets in their orbits. His laws of motion and gravity explained Kepler’s laws and why the planets orbit the Sun. The Sun-centered system now had a physical explanation."]],
sp:["The Copernican Revolution","The shift from an Earth-centered to a Sun-centered universe is called the Copernican Revolution. It took more than a century, through the work of Copernicus, Tycho Brahe, Kepler, Galileo and Newton. It changed not just astronomy but how people saw their place in the cosmos: Earth was one planet among many. The phrase Copernican revolution is now used for any idea that completely changes how we think."],
g:[["Geocentric","With Earth at the center"],["Heliocentric","With the Sun at the center"],["Epicycle","A small circle on a larger orbit in old models"],["Retrograde motion","A planet’s apparent backward movement"]],
f:["Copernicus also studied law and medicine and worked as a doctor.","A chemical element, copernicium, is named after him.","Kepler discovered that orbits are ellipses, not circles."]},
galileo:{s:[
["Falling objects","Aristotle taught that heavier objects fall faster than light ones. Galileo argued that, without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate. He tested this by rolling balls down slopes and timing them. Whether he really dropped balls from the Leaning Tower of Pisa is uncertain; the story comes from his student’s biography."],
["The pendulum","According to a famous story, the young Galileo watched a lamp swinging in Pisa Cathedral and timed it with his pulse. He found that a pendulum takes the same time to swing whether the swing is wide or narrow. This discovery later led to the invention of the pendulum clock by Christiaan Huygens in 1656."],
["Building telescopes","In 1609 Galileo heard of a Dutch spyglass and quickly built his own, improving it until it magnified about 20 times. He sold telescopes to merchants and the Venetian government, who saw their use for spotting ships. Then he pointed his instrument at the night sky, something few had done systematically before."],
["What he saw","Through his telescope Galileo saw mountains and craters on the Moon, countless stars in the Milky Way, four moons of Jupiter, the phases of Venus and dark spots on the Sun. The phases of Venus showed that it orbits the Sun, strong evidence against the Earth-centered model."],
["Conflict with the Church","In 1616 the Catholic Church declared the Sun-centered idea contrary to scripture and warned Galileo not to defend it. His 1632 Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems seemed to mock the Earth-centered view. In 1633 he was tried by the Inquisition, forced to recant and placed under house arrest for the rest of his life."],
["Two New Sciences","Under house arrest and going blind, Galileo wrote Two New Sciences, published in the Netherlands in 1638. It described his studies of motion and the strength of materials and laid the foundations of modern physics. He died in 1642, the year Isaac Newton was born. In 1992 Pope John Paul II acknowledged the Church had erred in condemning him."]],
sp:["“And yet it moves”","According to legend, after being forced to declare that Earth does not move, Galileo muttered, Eppur si muove, “And yet it moves.” There is no evidence he said it at the trial, which would have been very dangerous. The phrase appeared in writing more than a century later, though a painting from around the 1640s may include the words. True or not, it has become a symbol of standing by the truth under pressure."],
g:[["Pendulum","A weight swinging freely from a fixed point"],["Telescope","An instrument that makes distant things appear closer"],["Inquisition","A Church court that tried people for heresy"],["Recant","To publicly take back a belief"]],
f:["Galileo’s middle finger is displayed in a museum in Florence.","The Galileo spacecraft studied Jupiter, the planet whose moons he found.","Europe’s satellite navigation system is named Galileo."]},
goldage:{s:[
["Baghdad and beyond","From about the 8th to the 13th centuries, scholars across the Islamic world, from Spain to Central Asia, made great advances in science. Baghdad, founded in 762, became a center of learning under the Abbasid caliphs. Other centers included Cairo, Córdoba, Damascus, Samarkand and Bukhara."],
["Translation movement","Scholars translated works by Greek, Persian and Indian writers into Arabic, including Aristotle, Euclid, Ptolemy and Galen. Translators such as Hunayn ibn Ishaq were well paid. This saved many ancient works that might otherwise have been lost, and scholars added their own corrections and discoveries."],
["Al-Khwarizmi and numbers","Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad in the 800s, wrote a book that gave us the word algebra, from al-jabr, meaning restoring. Another of his books spread the Hindu numerals, including zero, which became our Hindu-Arabic numerals. The word algorithm comes from his name."],
["Ibn Sina","Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was a Persian doctor and philosopher who lived from about 980 to 1037. His Canon of Medicine collected the medical knowledge of his time and was used as a textbook in European universities until the 1600s. It described contagious diseases and how to test new medicines."],
["Instruments and observatories","Muslim astronomers built large observatories, such as at Maragha in Iran and Samarkand in Uzbekistan, and improved the astrolabe, an instrument for measuring the positions of stars. Many stars still have Arabic names, such as Aldebaran, Betelgeuse and Altair. They made accurate tables of planetary motion."],
["Chemistry and engineering","Jabir ibn Hayyan and later scholars developed laboratory techniques such as distillation and crystallization. Words such as alcohol, alkali and alchemy come from Arabic. Engineer al-Jazari, around 1206, described ingenious machines, including water clocks, pumps and robots powered by water."]],
sp:["Al-Jazari’s elephant clock","Ismail al-Jazari was an engineer who worked for a ruler in what is now Turkey. In 1206 he completed The Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices, describing about 100 machines with detailed drawings. His most famous design is the elephant clock, a water clock with a mechanical elephant, a scribe, a dragon and birds that moved and made sounds every half hour. It combined ideas from India, Persia, Egypt, Greece and China."],
g:[["Caliph","A ruler of the Islamic community"],["Astrolabe","An instrument for measuring star positions"],["Algorithm","A step-by-step method for solving a problem"],["Distillation","Separating liquids by boiling and cooling them"]],
f:["The first known university still operating, al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco, was founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri.","Paper-making spread from China to Baghdad in the 700s.","Many lunar craters are named after Muslim scholars."]},
alchemy:{s:[
["Ancient chemistry","Long before chemistry was a science, people used chemical skills to make metals, glass, dyes, perfumes, medicines and soap. Ancient Egyptians mummified bodies with salts and resins. Chinese alchemists searching for long life discovered gunpowder around the 9th century."],
["The philosopher’s stone","European alchemists sought the philosopher’s stone, a substance believed to turn cheap metals such as lead into gold and to give eternal life. They never succeeded, but their experiments developed equipment and methods, such as furnaces and flasks, that chemists still use. Isaac Newton spent years on alchemy."],
["Paracelsus","Paracelsus, a Swiss doctor of the 1500s, said alchemy should be used to make medicines rather than gold. He used chemicals such as mercury and argued that the dose makes the poison: any substance can be harmful in large amounts. He is sometimes called a father of toxicology."],
["Robert Boyle","In The Sceptical Chymist, published in 1661, the Irish-born scientist Robert Boyle attacked the old idea of four elements and argued that matter is made of tiny particles. He stressed careful experiments and published his methods. Boyle’s law describes how the pressure of a gas changes with its volume."],
["Lavoisier and oxygen","Antoine Lavoisier, a French chemist, showed in the 1770s that burning is a reaction with oxygen, not the release of an imaginary substance called phlogiston. He showed that mass is conserved in chemical reactions and created a modern system for naming chemicals. He was executed during the French Revolution in 1794."],
["Dalton and Mendeleev","In 1808 English teacher John Dalton proposed that each element is made of its own kind of atom, with its own weight. In 1869 Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the known elements by weight and properties in a periodic table, leaving gaps for elements not yet discovered and predicting their properties accurately."]],
sp:["Making gold for real","Alchemists dreamed of turning lead into gold. In the 20th century, physicists finally did it, in a way. Using particle accelerators, they knocked protons out of the nuclei of bismuth and other heavy atoms, creating tiny amounts of gold. In 2025, scientists at CERN reported that collisions of lead nuclei in the Large Hadron Collider briefly created gold atoms. The cost, however, is far more than the gold is worth."],
g:[["Element","A substance made of only one kind of atom"],["Compound","A substance of two or more elements bonded together"],["Phlogiston","An imaginary substance once thought released in burning"],["Periodic table","A chart of the elements arranged by properties"]],
f:["The chemical symbol for gold, Au, comes from the Latin aurum.","Lavoisier’s wife, Marie-Anne, helped with his experiments and drew his apparatus.","There are 118 known elements."]}
});

;
