// In-depth material (History of Science, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
pasteur:{s:[
["Spontaneous generation","For centuries, many people believed that living things such as maggots, mice and microbes could arise from nonliving matter, an idea called spontaneous generation. In 1668 Francesco Redi showed that maggots come from fly eggs, not rotting meat, but the question of microbes remained open."],
["The swan-neck flask","In 1859–1862 Louis Pasteur boiled broth in flasks with long curved S-shaped necks. Air could enter, but dust and microbes were trapped in the bend. The broth stayed clear for months. When he broke the neck, microbes got in and the broth spoiled. This showed microbes come from other microbes, not from nothing."],
["Fermentation","Pasteur showed that fermentation, which turns grape juice into wine and milk sour, is caused by living microorganisms such as yeast and bacteria. Different microbes produce different results, and unwanted microbes spoil wine and beer. Gentle heating to kill them became known as pasteurization."],
["Germs cause disease","Germ theory states that many diseases are caused by microbes invading the body. Before it, many doctors blamed bad air, called miasma. Pasteur’s work, Joseph Lister’s antiseptic surgery and Robert Koch’s discoveries of the germs causing anthrax, tuberculosis and cholera established germ theory by the 1880s."],
["The rabies vaccine","Rabies, spread by bites from infected animals, was almost always fatal once symptoms appeared. Pasteur developed a vaccine using weakened virus from dried rabbit spinal cords. In July 1885, nine-year-old Joseph Meister, bitten by a rabid dog, received the injections and survived, making Pasteur famous."],
["The Pasteur Institute","The Pasteur Institute was founded in Paris in 1887 with donations from around the world, and Pasteur worked there until his death in 1895. It became a leading center for research on infectious diseases, with a network of institutes around the world. Its scientists identified HIV in 1983."]],
sp:["Joseph Meister","On 6 July 1885, Joseph Meister, a nine-year-old boy from Alsace, was brought to Pasteur two days after being badly bitten by a rabid dog. Pasteur, who was not a medical doctor, was taking a great risk. Over ten days the boy received 13 injections of increasingly strong virus. He never developed rabies. Meister later worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute for many years."],
g:[["Germ theory","The idea that microbes cause many diseases"],["Fermentation","Chemical change caused by microbes such as yeast"],["Pasteurization","Gentle heating to kill harmful microbes"],["Spontaneous generation","The disproved idea that life arises from nonliving matter"]],
f:["Pasteur was trained as a chemist, not a doctor.","Pasteur studied crystals and discovered that some molecules have mirror-image forms.","Rabies still kills tens of thousands of people a year, mostly in Asia and Africa."]},
curie:{s:[
["Growing up in Warsaw","Maria Skłodowska was born in Warsaw, Poland, in 1867, when Poland was ruled by the Russian Empire. Her parents were teachers. Women could not attend university in Poland, so she studied secretly at the Flying University, an underground school, and worked as a governess to save money."],
["Meeting Pierre","In 1891 she moved to Paris and studied physics and mathematics at the Sorbonne, often living on bread and tea. In 1894 she met the physicist Pierre Curie, and they married in 1895. They worked together in a leaky shed with little equipment, processing metric tons of pitchblende ore by hand."],
["A new word","After Henri Becquerel discovered that uranium gives off mysterious rays in 1896, Marie Curie studied them and coined the word radioactivity. She found that the rays came from the atoms themselves, a revolutionary idea showing atoms are not unchangeable. In 1898 the Curies discovered polonium, named after Poland, and radium."],
["The first woman Nobel laureate","In 1903 Marie and Pierre Curie shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with Becquerel. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize. In 1911 she won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for isolating radium and polonium. She remains the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences."],
["Little Curies","During the First World War, Marie Curie developed mobile X-ray units, nicknamed little Curies, to help surgeons find bullets and shrapnel in wounded soldiers. She learned to drive and operated them at the front with her teenage daughter Irène. About a million soldiers were X-rayed."],
["The cost of radiation","The dangers of radiation were not understood. Curie carried test tubes of radium in her pockets and kept them in her desk. She died in 1934 of aplastic anemia, probably caused by radiation. Her notebooks are still radioactive and are kept in lead-lined boxes; visitors must sign a waiver to see them."]],
sp:["The Curie family","The Curies were a remarkable family of scientists. Pierre was killed by a horse-drawn wagon in 1906, and Marie took over his professorship, becoming the first woman professor at the Sorbonne. Their daughter Irène Joliot-Curie and her husband Frédéric won the 1935 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for creating artificial radioactivity. Their other daughter, Ève, wrote a famous biography of her mother. Altogether the family won five Nobel Prizes."],
g:[["Radioactivity","The release of energy from unstable atoms"],["Radium","A highly radioactive element discovered by the Curies"],["Polonium","A radioactive element named after Poland"],["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"]],
f:["The unit curie, for measuring radioactivity, is named after Marie and Pierre.","In 1995 the Curies’ remains were moved to the Panthéon in Paris.","The element curium is named after them."]},
currents:{s:[
["Electricity before power grids","In the early 1800s, electricity came only from batteries, invented by Alessandro Volta in 1800. In 1831 Michael Faraday discovered that moving a magnet near a coil of wire produces an electric current. This electromagnetic induction made generators possible and led to the age of electric power."],
["Lighting the city","In 1879 Thomas Edison’s team made a long-lasting carbon-filament light bulb. In 1882 Edison opened the Pearl Street Station in New York, one of the first power stations, supplying direct current, or DC, to about 85 customers. DC flowed in one direction and worked well for lamps and motors."],
["The problem with DC","DC at low voltage loses a lot of energy over long wires, so Edison’s power stations had to be within about a mile, or 1.6 km, of their customers. Every neighborhood would need its own power station. This made DC expensive for spreading electricity across cities and countryside."],
["Westinghouse and AC","Alternating current, AC, reverses direction many times a second. Using transformers, its voltage can be raised for efficient long-distance transmission and lowered for safe use in homes. Businessman George Westinghouse bought Nikola Tesla’s AC motor patents in 1888 and built AC systems."],
["The War of the Currents","Edison’s company campaigned against AC, warning that it was deadly. Supporters of DC staged public electrocutions of animals, and the first electric chair, used in 1890, ran on AC to link it with death. Despite this, AC was cheaper and more flexible, and it won."],
["Niagara Falls","In 1893 Westinghouse won the contract to light the Chicago World’s Fair with AC, dazzling visitors. In 1895–1896 a hydroelectric plant at Niagara Falls began sending AC power to Buffalo, about 40 km away. It proved that AC could deliver electricity over long distances, and AC became the world standard."]],
sp:["DC comes back","AC won the War of the Currents, but DC never disappeared. Batteries, phones, computers, LED lights and solar panels all use DC, so devices contain adapters to convert AC from the wall. Today, high-voltage DC lines are used to send power over very long distances or under the sea, where they lose less energy than AC. China’s longest lines carry power more than 3,000 kilometers from its western provinces to its eastern cities."],
g:[["Direct current","Electric current that flows one way"],["Alternating current","Electric current that changes direction rapidly"],["Transformer","A device that changes voltage"],["Generator","A machine that turns motion into electricity"]],
f:["Japan is split between 50 hertz in the east and 60 hertz in the west.","Edison held over 1,000 patents.","The unit of magnetic field strength, the tesla, is named after Nikola Tesla."]},
penicil:{s:[
["Infections of the past","Before antibiotics, a scratch, sore throat or childbirth could lead to deadly infection. Pneumonia, meningitis, tuberculosis and infected wounds killed millions. In wars, many more soldiers died of infections than from their wounds. Doctors could do little except clean wounds and hope."],
["The moldy dish","In September 1928, Alexander Fleming returned from holiday to his laboratory at St Mary’s Hospital in London. On one dish of Staphylococcus bacteria, a blue-green mold had grown, and around it the bacteria had died. Fleming identified the mold as Penicillium and named the substance it made penicillin."],
["Florey and Chain","Fleming could not purify penicillin, and for about ten years little was done with it. In 1939 Howard Florey, Ernst Chain, Norman Heatley and their team at Oxford found ways to extract and purify it. In 1940 they showed it cured infections in mice. They worked in a lab full of improvised equipment, even bedpans."],
["Albert Alexander","In 1941 a police constable, Albert Alexander, became the first patient treated with purified penicillin. He had a severe infection from a scratch. He improved dramatically, but supplies ran out, even though some penicillin was recovered from his urine, and he died. It proved penicillin worked; the challenge was making enough."],
["The moldy cantaloupe","American laboratories searched for a mold that produced more penicillin. In 1943 a strain from a moldy cantaloupe found in a market in Peoria, Illinois, proved far more productive. Using deep tanks, companies produced enough penicillin to treat Allied soldiers by D-Day in 1944. Fleming, Florey and Chain won the 1945 Nobel Prize."],
["Using antibiotics wisely","Antibiotics kill bacteria but do not work against viruses such as colds and flu. Using them unnecessarily, or not finishing a prescribed course, can help resistant bacteria survive and spread. Resistant superbugs are a growing threat. Doctors urge patients to take antibiotics only when prescribed and exactly as directed."]],
sp:["Dorothy Hodgkin","To make penicillin artificially, chemists needed to know its structure. British chemist Dorothy Hodgkin used X-ray crystallography, firing X-rays through crystals and studying the patterns, to work out the arrangement of atoms in penicillin in 1945. She later found the structures of vitamin B12 and insulin. She won the 1964 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, the only British woman to win a science Nobel to date."],
g:[["Antibiotic","A medicine that kills or stops bacteria"],["Mold","A fungus that grows in threads"],["Resistance","When bacteria are no longer killed by a drug"],["Superbug","A bacterium resistant to many antibiotics"]],
f:["Penicillin is still widely used, though many bacteria now resist it.","Some of Fleming’s original mold has been sold at auction.","The word antibiotic was popularized by Selman Waksman, who discovered streptomycin."]},
hubble:{s:[
["Edwin Hubble","Edwin Hubble was born in Missouri, United States, in 1889. He was a talented athlete and studied law at Oxford as a Rhodes Scholar before turning to astronomy. After serving in the First World War, he joined the Mount Wilson Observatory in California in 1919, home of the 100-inch Hooker telescope, then the largest in the world."],
["The Great Debate","In 1920 astronomers Harlow Shapley and Heber Curtis held a famous debate about whether spiral nebulae were clouds within the Milky Way or separate galaxies far beyond it. Shapley thought the Milky Way was the whole universe. The question could only be settled by measuring the distances to the nebulae."],
["Henrietta Leavitt’s yardstick","In 1912 Henrietta Swan Leavitt, working at Harvard Observatory, discovered that Cepheid variable stars that are truly brighter pulse more slowly. By measuring how fast a Cepheid pulses, astronomers could work out its true brightness and so its distance. Her discovery gave astronomers a cosmic measuring tape."],
["Andromeda is a galaxy","In 1923 Hubble found a Cepheid in the Andromeda nebula. Using Leavitt’s method, he showed it was far beyond the Milky Way, about 900,000 light-years by his calculation; the modern figure is about 2.5 million. Andromeda was a separate galaxy, and the universe was vastly larger than anyone had thought."],
["Running away","Vesto Slipher had found that most galaxies’ light is shifted toward red, meaning they are moving away. In 1929 Hubble, with Milton Humason, compared these speeds with distances and found that the farther a galaxy is, the faster it recedes. Belgian Georges Lemaître had reached a similar result in 1927, so it is now called the Hubble–Lemaître law."],
["Hubble’s legacy","Hubble’s discoveries showed that the Milky Way is one of billions of galaxies and that the universe is expanding, supporting the Big Bang theory. Einstein visited him in 1931 and abandoned his idea of a static universe. Hubble died in 1953. He never won a Nobel Prize, as astronomy was not then considered part of physics."]],
sp:["The Hubble tension","The Hubble constant measures how fast the universe is expanding. Measurements using nearby stars and supernovas give about 73 kilometers per second per megaparsec, but measurements from the cosmic microwave background give about 67. This disagreement, called the Hubble tension, has grown stronger as measurements improve, including from the James Webb Space Telescope. It may hint at new physics, and astronomers are working hard to solve it."],
g:[["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust, once also used for galaxies"],["Cepheid","A star that pulses regularly in brightness"],["Hubble constant","A number for how fast the universe is expanding"],["Recede","To move away"]],
f:["Hubble once worked as a high school teacher and basketball coach.","Henrietta Leavitt was deaf and was paid very little for her work.","Asteroid 2069 Hubble is named after him."]}
});

;
