// In-depth material (Mathematics, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
zero:{s:[
["Tally marks","The earliest evidence of counting may be notched bones. The Ishango bone, found in what is now the Democratic Republic of the Congo, is about 20,000 years old and carries groups of carved notches. Tally marks, grouped in fives, are still used to keep score."],
["Number systems of the past","Ancient peoples wrote numbers in many ways. Egyptians used separate symbols for 1, 10, 100 and so on, repeating them as needed. Romans used letters such as I, V, X, L, C, D and M. Babylonians counted in 60s, which is why an hour has 60 minutes and a circle 360 degrees."],
["Brahmagupta’s rules","In 628 CE the Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote down rules for calculating with zero as a number, such as that a number minus itself is zero and that a number multiplied by zero is zero. He also wrote rules for negative numbers, which he called debts, and positive numbers, called fortunes."],
["Hindu-Arabic numerals","Indian mathematicians developed nine digits plus zero, with a place-value system. Arab scholars adopted them, and Leonardo of Pisa, known as Fibonacci, introduced them to Europe in his book Liber Abaci in 1202. They replaced Roman numerals for calculation over the following centuries because they made arithmetic much easier."],
["Dividing by zero","Dividing by zero is undefined. If you share 6 sweets among 0 people, the question has no sensible answer. Mathematically, if 6 ÷ 0 equaled some number, then that number times 0 would equal 6, which is impossible. Calculators show an error, and computer programs can crash if they try it."],
["Words for numbers","Number words reveal history. In many languages the word for five is linked to the hand: in Austronesian languages, spoken from Madagascar to Hawaii, five is lima, which also means hand. French counts some numbers in twenties, as in quatre-vingts, four twenties, for 80, a trace of an older system also found in Welsh and Danish. Some Amazonian languages have words only for one, two and many."]],
sp:["The Bakhshali manuscript","The Bakhshali manuscript is an ancient Indian mathematical text written on birch bark, found buried in a field near Peshawar in 1881 and now kept in Oxford. It uses a dot as a placeholder for zero. In 2017 radiocarbon dating suggested that some of its pages may date to the 3rd or 4th century CE, which would make it among the oldest known uses of a zero symbol, though scholars still debate the dates."],
g:[["Digit","A single symbol used to write numbers"],["Place value","The value a digit has from its position"],["Integer","A whole number, positive, negative or zero"],["Undefined","Having no meaningful value"]],
f:["The word zero comes from the Arabic sifr, meaning empty, which also gave us cipher.","Ancient Romans had no numeral for zero.","Zero is an even number."]},
euclid:{s:[
["Euclid of Alexandria","Very little is known about Euclid’s life. He taught in Alexandria, Egypt, around 300 BCE, at the time of King Ptolemy I. According to a story, when the king asked for an easier way to learn geometry, Euclid replied that there is no royal road to geometry."],
["Thirteen books","The Elements is divided into 13 books. They cover plane geometry, ratios and proportions, number theory, including prime numbers, and solid geometry, ending with the five regular solids. Euclid collected and organized the work of earlier mathematicians and added proofs of his own."],
["The five postulates","Euclid began with five basic assumptions, or postulates. For example, a straight line can be drawn between any two points, and all right angles are equal. The fifth, the parallel postulate, says in effect that through a point not on a line exactly one parallel line can be drawn. Mathematicians spent 2,000 years trying to prove it from the others."],
["The Pythagorean theorem","One of the most famous results in the Elements is the Pythagorean theorem: in a right triangle, the square on the longest side equals the sum of the squares on the other two sides, a² + b² = c². Babylonians knew examples a thousand years before Pythagoras. Builders use it to make sure corners are square."],
["Non-Euclidean geometry","In the 1800s, Nikolai Lobachevsky, János Bolyai and Carl Friedrich Gauss discovered geometries in which the parallel postulate is false. On a sphere, for instance, lines are great circles, and triangles have angles adding up to more than 180°. Einstein used curved geometry to describe gravity."],
["Geometry and art","Artists use geometry for perspective, proportion and pattern. Islamic art features complex geometric tilings. Renaissance painters used vanishing points to create depth. Weavers around the world, from the Navajo of North America to the Kente weavers of Ghana and the T’boli of Mindanao, create geometric patterns in textiles."]],
sp:["The most published textbook","After the Bible, Euclid’s Elements may be the most widely printed book in the Western world. It was translated into Arabic, then Latin, and first printed in Venice in 1482. For more than 2,000 years it was the standard geometry textbook. Abraham Lincoln studied it to sharpen his reasoning as a young lawyer, and Einstein called a geometry book he read at 12 his holy little geometry book."],
g:[["Postulate","A basic assumption accepted without proof"],["Theorem","A statement proved from postulates"],["Right angle","An angle of exactly 90 degrees"],["Parallel","Lines that never meet"]],
f:["There are exactly five regular solids, also called Platonic solids.","The word geometry means Earth measurement in Greek.","Euclid’s Elements contains 465 propositions."]},
prob:{s:[
["Measuring chance","Probability measures how likely something is, on a scale from 0, impossible, to 1, certain. It can be written as a fraction, decimal or percentage. The chance of a fair coin landing heads is 1/2, 0.5 or 50%. The chance of rolling a 6 on a fair die is 1/6."],
["Pascal and Fermat","Modern probability began in 1654, when French mathematicians Blaise Pascal and Pierre de Fermat exchanged letters about a gambling problem: how to divide the stakes fairly if a game is stopped before it ends. Their solutions laid the foundations of probability theory."],
["Combining events","To find the chance of two independent events both happening, multiply their probabilities. The chance of getting two heads in a row is 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4. To find the chance of one or another of two events that cannot happen together, add them: the chance of rolling a 1 or a 2 is 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3."],
["Lotteries and odds","The chance of winning a big lottery prize is tiny. In a lottery where a player must match six numbers from 1 to 58, there are over 40 million possible combinations, and in the American Powerball the odds of winning the jackpot are about 1 in 292 million. Lotteries make money because, on average, players pay more than they win back."],
["Risk and insurance","Insurance companies use probability to estimate how often events such as accidents, fires or illnesses will happen. They set premiums so that the money collected from many people covers the claims of the few who suffer losses. Weather forecasters also use probability, as in a 70% chance of rain."],
["The Monty Hall problem","In a game show, you choose one of three doors; behind one is a car, and behind the others goats. The host, who knows where the car is, opens another door to show a goat and offers you a switch. Surprisingly, switching wins two-thirds of the time, because your first pick was right only one-third of the time."]],
sp:["The birthday problem","How many people must be in a room for there to be a better than even chance that two share a birthday? Most people guess around 180. The answer is just 23. With 23 people, there are 253 different pairs, each with a small chance of a match, which adds up quickly. With 70 people, the chance rises to over 99.9%. The puzzle shows how poor our instincts about probability can be."],
g:[["Probability","A measure of how likely an event is"],["Odds","The ratio of chances for and against"],["Independent events","Events that do not affect each other"],["Random","Without a predictable pattern"]],
f:["A shuffled deck of cards has more possible orders than there are atoms on Earth.","Lightning strikes Earth about 40 to 50 times a second, but the chance of a person being struck is very small.","Probability is used to design fair games and to check whether results are due to chance."]},
prime:{s:[
["What makes a prime","A prime number is a whole number greater than 1 that can be divided evenly only by 1 and itself. The first primes are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23 and 29. A number that has other divisors, such as 12, is called composite. The number 1 is neither prime nor composite."],
["The only even prime","Two is the only even prime number, because every other even number can be divided by 2. All other primes are odd. Apart from 2 and 5, every prime ends in 1, 3, 7 or 9. These clues help when checking whether a large number might be prime."],
["Euclid’s proof","Around 300 BCE, Euclid proved that there are infinitely many primes. Suppose there were a last prime. Multiply all the primes together and add 1. The new number cannot be divided by any of those primes, since there is always a remainder of 1, so either it is prime or it has a prime factor not on the list. Either way, the list was incomplete."],
["Prime factors","The fundamental theorem of arithmetic states that every whole number greater than 1 can be written as a product of primes in exactly one way, apart from order. For example, 60 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5. Prime factors are useful for simplifying fractions and finding common multiples."],
["Primes and secrets","Online security uses primes. In RSA encryption, invented in 1977, a computer multiplies two very large primes to make a public key. Multiplying is easy, but splitting the result back into its prime factors is extremely hard for ordinary computers. This protects bank transactions, messages and passwords."],
["Primes in nature","Some cicadas in North America spend 13 or 17 years underground before emerging all at once. Scientists suggest that prime-numbered cycles help them avoid predators with shorter cycles, because primes rarely line up with other numbers. Whether this explanation is correct is still debated."]],
sp:["Searching for giant primes","The largest known primes are Mersenne primes, of the form 2ⁿ − 1. Since 1996 volunteers around the world have searched for them with the Great Internet Mersenne Prime Search, GIMPS, using spare computer time. In October 2024, Luke Durant, a former engineer using cloud-based graphics processors, found 2^136,279,841 − 1, a number with more than 41 million digits, the largest prime then known."],
g:[["Prime","A number divisible only by 1 and itself"],["Composite","A number with more than two factors"],["Factor","A number that divides another exactly"],["Encryption","Scrambling information to keep it secret"]],
f:["There are 25 primes less than 100.","Primes that differ by 2, such as 11 and 13, are called twin primes.","A prize of $250,000 has been offered for finding a prime with a billion digits."]},
calc:{s:[
["The mathematics of change","Calculus is the branch of mathematics that studies how things change. It deals with rates of change, such as speed, and with accumulation, such as total distance traveled or area under a curve. Calculus is essential to physics, engineering, economics and biology."],
["Newton and Leibniz","Isaac Newton in England and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in Germany developed calculus independently in the late 1600s. Newton used it to explain the motion of planets. Leibniz published first, in 1684, and created the notation still used today, such as dx and the integral sign ∫, a long S for sum."],
["The priority dispute","A bitter argument broke out over who had invented calculus first. Newton had worked out his methods in the 1660s but did not publish them for years. In 1712 the Royal Society, headed by Newton himself, ruled in his favor. Historians now agree both developed it independently."],
["Slopes and speed","A derivative measures how fast one quantity changes compared with another, such as how fast a car’s position changes over time, its speed. On a graph, the derivative is the slope of the curve at a point. Derivatives help find maximums and minimums, such as the shape of can that uses least metal."],
["Areas and totals","An integral adds up infinitely many tiny pieces to find a total, such as the area under a curve or the volume of a strange shape. If you know how fast water flows into a tank at every moment, integration tells you how much water is in it. Integration and differentiation are opposites."],
["Calculus at work","Engineers use calculus to design bridges, cars and aircraft. Doctors use it to model how medicines spread through the body and how diseases spread in populations. Economists use it to study growth and costs. Weather forecasts and video game physics also depend on it."]],
sp:["Maria Gaetana Agnesi","Maria Gaetana Agnesi, born in Milan in 1718, wrote one of the first textbooks to explain both differential and integral calculus clearly, published in 1748. It was translated into English and used for decades. She was the first woman appointed as a professor of mathematics at a university, in Bologna, though she may never have taught there. A curve she studied, the witch of Agnesi, got its name from a mistranslation."],
g:[["Derivative","The rate at which something changes"],["Integral","The total built from adding tiny parts"],["Slope","The steepness of a line or curve"],["Rate of change","How fast one quantity changes compared with another"]],
f:["The word calculus comes from the Latin for small pebble, used for counting.","Archimedes used methods similar to integration over 2,200 years ago.","Calculus is needed to calculate the orbits of satellites."]}
});

;
