// In-depth material (Religion and Philosophy, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
socr:{s:[
["Athens in its golden age","In the 400s BCE, Athens was a wealthy city-state and an early democracy, where male citizens voted directly on laws. It was home to playwrights, historians, sculptors and architects who built the Parthenon. In this lively city, people debated in the agora, the marketplace, and philosophy flourished."],
["The trial of Socrates","In 399 BCE Socrates was put on trial, accused of disrespecting the city’s gods and corrupting the young. A jury of 501 citizens found him guilty. In his defense speech, recorded by Plato in the Apology, he said that the unexamined life is not worth living. He was sentenced to death by drinking hemlock, a poison."],
["The Academy","Around 387 BCE Plato founded the Academy in a grove outside Athens, often called the first institution of higher learning in the Western world. Students studied mathematics, astronomy, politics and philosophy. It is said that above its entrance was written, Let no one ignorant of geometry enter. The word academy comes from it."],
["The cave","In Plato’s Republic, Socrates describes prisoners chained in a cave, seeing only shadows on a wall and thinking them real. One prisoner escapes and sees the sunlit world. Returning, he is mocked. The allegory represents the journey from ignorance to knowledge, and the philosopher’s duty to help others see."],
["Aristotle’s Lyceum","Aristotle studied at Plato’s Academy for about 20 years, then tutored the young Alexander the Great. In 335 BCE he founded his own school, the Lyceum, in Athens. He often taught while walking, so his followers were called Peripatetics, meaning those who walk about. He wrote on logic, ethics, politics, poetry and biology."],
["The golden mean","Aristotle taught that virtue lies in a middle path between two extremes. Courage, for example, lies between cowardice and recklessness, and generosity between stinginess and wastefulness. The aim of life, he said, is eudaimonia, often translated as flourishing or happiness, achieved by living virtuously with reason."]],
sp:["Logic","Aristotle created the first formal system of logic, a way of testing whether reasoning is valid. His best-known form is the syllogism: All humans are mortal; Socrates is a human; therefore Socrates is mortal. If the first two statements are true, the conclusion must be true. Aristotle’s logic was the standard for about 2,000 years and laid groundwork for mathematics, law and computer science."],
g:[["Philosophy","The love of wisdom; the study of basic questions"],["Dialogue","A written conversation exploring ideas"],["Syllogism","An argument with two premises and a conclusion"],["Virtue","Good character or moral excellence"]],
f:["Socrates left no writings; we know him through Plato and Xenophon.","Plato’s real name may have been Aristocles; Plato may have been a nickname meaning broad.","Aristotle classified hundreds of animals and studied marine life."]},
buddh:{s:[
["Siddhartha’s birth","Siddhartha Gautama was born, according to tradition, in Lumbini, now in Nepal, in the 500s or 400s BCE, to a ruling family of the Shakya clan. Legends say a sage predicted he would become either a great king or a great spiritual teacher. His father tried to shield him from all suffering."],
["Enlightenment","After years of seeking, Siddhartha sat beneath a fig tree, later called the Bodhi tree, at Bodh Gaya in India and vowed to meditate until he understood the truth. He achieved enlightenment and became the Buddha, meaning awakened one. He then taught for about 45 years across northern India."],
["The Four Noble Truths","The Buddha’s first teaching set out four noble truths: life involves suffering or dissatisfaction, dukkha; suffering arises from craving and attachment; suffering can end; and the way to end it is the Noble Eightfold Path. The ending of suffering is called nirvana, often described as the blowing out of a flame."],
["Schools of Buddhism","Theravada, the way of the elders, is followed mainly in Sri Lanka, Myanmar, Thailand, Laos and Cambodia. Mahayana, the great vehicle, spread to China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam and includes Zen and Pure Land. Vajrayana, or Tibetan Buddhism, is practiced in Tibet, Bhutan and Mongolia. The Dalai Lama is a leading Tibetan teacher."],
["Monks and nuns","Buddhist monks and nuns, the sangha, follow many rules, shaving their heads, wearing robes and owning few possessions. In Theravada countries they walk each morning to receive food from laypeople, who gain merit by giving. Many young men in Thailand and Myanmar spend a period as monks."],
["Buddhism around the world","About 500 million people follow Buddhism, most in East and Southeast Asia. Theravada Buddhism is practiced in Sri Lanka, Thailand, Myanmar, Laos and Cambodia, while Mahayana schools, including Zen, spread through China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam. Tibetan Buddhism is led by the Dalai Lama. Since the 1900s, Buddhist meditation has become popular in Europe and the Americas."]],
sp:["Borobudur","Borobudur, on the island of Java in Indonesia, is the largest Buddhist temple in the world. Built in the 800s, it rises in nine stacked terraces carved with more than 2,600 relief panels telling the life of the Buddha and Buddhist teachings, topped by 72 bell-shaped stupas. Abandoned for centuries and covered by volcanic ash and jungle, it was restored with UNESCO’s help in the 1970s and 1980s."],
g:[["Enlightenment","Awakening to the true nature of reality"],["Nirvana","The ending of suffering and rebirth"],["Dharma","The Buddha’s teaching"],["Sangha","The community of Buddhist monks and nuns"]],
f:["There are an estimated 500 million Buddhists worldwide.","Vesak celebrates the Buddha’s birth, enlightenment and death.","A descendant of the original Bodhi tree still grows at Bodh Gaya."]},
confu:{s:[
["Kong Fuzi","Confucius is the Latin form of Kong Fuzi, Master Kong. He was born in 551 BCE in the state of Lu, in eastern China, into a family of modest nobility. His father died when he was young, and he was raised by his mother. He was largely self-taught and began teaching in his twenties."],
["Wandering teacher","Confucius held minor government posts but was frustrated that rulers ignored his advice. For about 14 years he traveled between states with his disciples, seeking a ruler who would put his ideas into practice. He returned to Lu and spent his last years teaching and editing classic texts. He died in 479 BCE."],
["Ren and li","Two central ideas of Confucius are ren, often translated as humaneness or benevolence, and li, ritual or proper conduct. Ren means caring for others; li means behaving correctly in each relationship and situation. He also taught a version of the golden rule: do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself."],
["The junzi","Confucius described the ideal person as a junzi, a gentleman or noble person, defined by character rather than birth. The junzi studies, practices virtue, is loyal and sincere, and leads by moral example. Confucius believed that if rulers were virtuous, the people would follow them naturally."],
["Mencius and Xunzi","After Confucius, his ideas were developed by later thinkers. Mencius, in the 300s BCE, taught that human nature is basically good and must be nurtured, like a sprout. Xunzi, in the 200s BCE, argued human nature tends toward selfishness and must be shaped by education and ritual."],
["Neo-Confucianism","From the 1000s, scholars such as Zhu Xi combined Confucian ethics with ideas from Buddhism and Taoism, creating Neo-Confucianism. Zhu Xi’s selection of the Four Books became the basis of the Chinese imperial examinations from 1313 until 1905. Neo-Confucianism also shaped Korea, Japan and Vietnam."]],
sp:["The imperial examinations","For about 1,300 years, China chose many of its government officials through competitive examinations. Candidates spent years memorizing the Confucian classics and writing essays on them. Exams were held in walled compounds of tiny cells, where candidates stayed for days. Passing the highest levels brought great honor to a family. The system was abolished in 1905, but it influenced civil service exams in other countries."],
g:[["Ren","Humaneness or kindness toward others"],["Li","Ritual and proper conduct"],["Junzi","An ideal person of virtue"],["Filial piety","Respect and care for parents and elders"]],
f:["Confucius’s birthday, 28 September, is celebrated as Teachers’ Day in Taiwan.","Kong family descendants have kept genealogies for more than 80 generations.","The Confucius Temple in Qufu has been rebuilt and expanded many times."]},
relig:{s:[
["What is religion?","A religion is a system of beliefs and practices, usually concerning the sacred or divine, that gives followers meaning, moral guidance and community. Religions often include sacred texts, prayer or meditation, rituals, festivals, places of worship and teachings about how to live and what happens after death."],
["Monotheism and polytheism","Monotheistic religions, such as Judaism, Christianity and Islam, believe in one God. Polytheistic traditions honor many gods and goddesses, as in ancient Greek and Egyptian religion and some forms of Hinduism. Some traditions, such as Buddhism, do not focus on a creator god at all."],
["Rites of passage","Religions mark important stages of life with ceremonies. Many Christians are baptized as babies. Jewish young people celebrate bar or bat mitzvah. Hindus perform sacred thread ceremonies. Weddings and funerals are among the most important rituals in almost every faith."],
["Pilgrimage","Many religions encourage pilgrimage, a journey to a holy place. Muslims travel to Mecca for the Hajj; Hindus bathe in the Ganges at Varanasi; Christians visit Jerusalem, Rome and Lourdes, and millions gather at the Basilica of Our Lady of Guadalupe in Mexico City each December."],
["Ethics and charity","Most religions teach compassion and care for others. Versions of the golden rule appear in many faiths. Charity is important: Muslims give zakat, Jews tzedakah, and Christians alms, while Sikhs serve free meals in community kitchens called langar. Religious groups run schools, hospitals and relief agencies."],
["Interfaith dialogue","Interfaith dialogue brings people of different faiths together to understand each other and work for peace. Groups bring together Christian, Muslim, Jewish, Hindu and Buddhist leaders in many countries. The World Interfaith Harmony Week, proposed by Jordan and adopted by the UN in 2010, is held in the first week of February."]],
sp:["Sikhism","Sikhism was founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab region of South Asia in the 1400s and 1500s. Sikhs believe in one God and the equality of all people, and they follow the teachings of ten Gurus and their holy book, the Guru Granth Sahib. The Golden Temple in Amritsar welcomes visitors of all faiths and serves free meals to tens of thousands of people daily. There are about 25 to 30 million Sikhs, with large communities in Canada, Britain and the United States."],
g:[["Monotheism","Belief in one God"],["Polytheism","Belief in many gods"],["Pilgrimage","A journey to a holy place"],["Ritual","A set of actions performed in a ceremony"]],
f:["About 85% of the world’s people identify with a religion.","About one person in seven in the world has no religious affiliation.","Many festivals, such as Christmas, Eid and Diwali, are public holidays in multicultural countries."]},
hindu:{s:[
["The Indus and the Vedas","Hinduism has no single founder. Its roots may reach back to the Indus Valley civilization and to the Vedas, sacred hymns composed in Sanskrit from around 1500 BCE and passed on by memory for centuries. It is often called the oldest living major religion. Many Hindus call it Sanatana Dharma, the eternal way."],
["Many paths","Hinduism includes a wide variety of beliefs and practices. Hindus may follow the path of devotion, bhakti; of knowledge, jnana; of selfless action, karma; or of meditation, yoga. Major traditions focus on Vishnu, Shiva or the goddess Devi. Many Hindus see the many gods as forms of one ultimate reality, Brahman."],
["Dharma, karma and moksha","Dharma means duty, right action or the order of the universe. Karma is the idea that actions have consequences in this and future lives. The soul, atman, is reborn in a cycle called samsara. The final goal is moksha, release from the cycle of rebirth."],
["The Bhagavad Gita","The Bhagavad Gita, part of the epic Mahabharata, is a conversation between the warrior prince Arjuna and the god Krishna, who serves as his charioteer, on the eve of battle. Krishna teaches him to do his duty without attachment to results. It is one of the most widely read Hindu texts."],
["Festivals","Diwali, the festival of lights, celebrates the victory of light over darkness with lamps, fireworks and sweets. Holi, the festival of colors, welcomes spring with people throwing colored powder. Navaratri honors the goddess over nine nights. The Kumbh Mela, held at sacred rivers, draws tens of millions of pilgrims."],
["Hinduism in the world","There are about 1.2 billion Hindus, around 15% of the world’s population, most in India and Nepal. Large communities live in Bangladesh, Indonesia’s Bali, Sri Lanka, Mauritius, Malaysia, the Caribbean, Fiji, Britain and North America."]],
sp:["Angkor Wat","Angkor Wat in Cambodia is the largest religious monument in the world, covering more than 160 hectares. It was built in the early 1100s by King Suryavarman II as a Hindu temple dedicated to Vishnu, and it later became a Buddhist temple. Its five towers represent Mount Meru, home of the gods. Its walls are carved with scenes from the Ramayana and Mahabharata, and it appears on Cambodia’s national flag."],
g:[["Dharma","Duty, right action and cosmic order"],["Karma","The effect of a person’s actions"],["Moksha","Release from the cycle of rebirth"],["Avatar","A god appearing in earthly form"]],
f:["The word yoga means union in Sanskrit.","Many words in the languages of Southeast Asia come from Sanskrit.","The cow is considered sacred by many Hindus."]}
});

;
