// In-depth material (Religion and Philosophy, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
islam:{s:[
["Meaning of Islam","The word Islam means submission or surrender to God, and a Muslim is one who submits. Muslims believe in one God, Allah in Arabic, and that Muhammad, born in Mecca around 570 CE, was the last of a line of prophets that includes Abraham, Moses and Jesus."],
["The Hijra","As Muhammad’s message spread, he and his followers faced persecution in Mecca. In 622 CE they moved to the city of Yathrib, later called Medina. This migration, the Hijra, marks the start of the Islamic calendar. By the time of his death in 632, much of Arabia had accepted Islam."],
["Sunni and Shia","After Muhammad’s death, disagreements arose over who should lead the community. Sunni Muslims, about 85 to 90% of Muslims, accepted the elected caliphs. Shia Muslims believe leadership should have passed to Ali, Muhammad’s cousin and son-in-law, and his descendants. Most Shia live in Iran, Iraq, Bahrain and Azerbaijan."],
["Mosques","A mosque, masjid in Arabic, is a place of prayer. It usually has a prayer hall, a niche called the mihrab showing the direction of Mecca, and often a minaret, from which the call to prayer is made. Worshippers remove their shoes and wash before praying. Friday midday prayers are especially important."],
["Islamic art","Islamic religious art usually avoids images of people and animals and instead uses geometric patterns, flowing plant designs called arabesques and beautiful calligraphy of Quranic verses. Famous Islamic buildings include the Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem, the Alhambra in Spain and the Taj Mahal in India."],
["Eid and the Islamic calendar","The Islamic calendar is lunar, with 12 months of 29 or 30 days, so it is about 11 days shorter than the solar year and its festivals move through the seasons. Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan, and Eid al-Adha honors Abraham’s willingness to sacrifice his son. Both are major holidays across the Muslim world."]],
sp:["The Hajj","The Hajj is the pilgrimage to Mecca, required once in a lifetime of every Muslim who is physically and financially able. Each year about 2 million pilgrims from around the world gather in the Islamic month of Dhu al-Hijjah. Men wear simple white garments, so rich and poor look alike. Pilgrims walk seven times around the Kaaba, stand in prayer on the plain of Arafat and symbolically stone pillars representing temptation."],
g:[["Mosque","A Muslim place of worship"],["Imam","A leader of prayers in a mosque"],["Ramadan","The month of fasting in Islam"],["Hijra","Muhammad’s migration from Mecca to Medina"]],
f:["Islam is the world’s second-largest religion, with about 2 billion followers.","Indonesia has the largest Muslim population of any country.","Indonesia has the largest Muslim population of any country."]},
christ:{s:[
["The early Church","After the death of Jesus, his followers, led by apostles such as Peter and Paul, spread his teachings across the Roman Empire. Early Christians were often persecuted. In 313 the Edict of Milan allowed Christians to worship freely, and in 380 Christianity became the official religion of the Roman Empire."],
["The Bible","The Christian Bible has two parts. The Old Testament contains the Hebrew scriptures shared with Judaism. The New Testament contains the four Gospels about the life of Jesus, the Acts of the Apostles, letters and the Book of Revelation. The Bible has been translated, in whole or in part, into over 3,000 languages."],
["East and West","In 1054 the Church split into the Catholic Church in the West, led by the Pope in Rome, and the Eastern Orthodox Church, centered in Constantinople. This Great Schism followed long disagreements over authority and practice. Orthodox Christianity is strong in Greece, Russia, Ukraine and Eastern Europe."],
["The Reformation","In 1517 the German monk Martin Luther criticized practices of the Catholic Church, including the selling of indulgences. His protest led to the Protestant Reformation and new churches, such as the Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican and later Methodist and Baptist churches. The printing press helped spread the new ideas quickly."],
["Christmas and Easter","Christmas celebrates the birth of Jesus, and Easter his resurrection, the most important Christian festival. Easter is preceded by Lent, 40 days of prayer and fasting, and Holy Week, marked in many countries by processions, such as those of Seville in Spain and Antigua in Guatemala."],
["Christianity today","With about 2.4 billion followers, Christianity is the world’s largest religion. Catholics make up about half. Christianity is growing fastest in Africa, which now has more Christians than any other continent."]],
sp:["Christmas around the world","Christmas is celebrated in different ways around the world. In Mexico, people act out Mary and Joseph’s search for shelter in nine nights of posadas. In the Philippines, the season begins in September and nine dawn Masses lead up to Christmas. Germans light Advent wreaths, Ethiopian Orthodox Christians celebrate on 7 January, and Australians often celebrate with a barbecue on the beach."],
g:[["Gospel","An account of the life and teachings of Jesus"],["Apostle","One of the first followers sent to spread the faith"],["Sacrament","A sacred ritual such as baptism"],["Denomination","A branch of a religion"]],
f:["Africa now has more Christians than any other continent.","The Vatican is the world’s smallest independent state.","Pope Leo XIV, elected in May 2025, is the first pope from the United States."]},
judaism:{s:[
["The covenant","Judaism is one of the oldest monotheistic religions, over 3,000 years old. Jews believe God made a covenant, a sacred agreement, with Abraham and his descendants, and later gave the Torah to Moses at Mount Sinai. In return, the people would follow God’s commandments."],
["The Torah","The Torah consists of the first five books of the Hebrew Bible: Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy. In synagogues it is handwritten on a parchment scroll by a trained scribe and read aloud through the year. The whole Hebrew Bible is called the Tanakh. The Talmud collects centuries of discussion and law."],
["Shabbat","Shabbat, the Sabbath, begins at sunset on Friday and ends at nightfall on Saturday. It is a day of rest, prayer and family meals, remembering that God rested on the seventh day of creation. Families light candles, bless wine and braided challah bread, and many refrain from work."],
["Kosher food","Many Jews follow dietary laws called kashrut. Kosher food includes only certain animals, such as cows and sheep, slaughtered in a specific way; pork and shellfish are forbidden. Meat and dairy are not eaten together. Kosher means fit or proper in Hebrew."],
["The Holocaust","During the Second World War, Nazi Germany murdered about 6 million Jews, two-thirds of Europe’s Jews, in the Holocaust, also called the Shoah. Millions of others were also killed. The Holocaust is remembered on International Holocaust Remembrance Day, 27 January, the anniversary of the liberation of Auschwitz."],
["Israel and the diaspora","The modern state of Israel was established in 1948, and about 7 million Jews now live there. Large communities also live in the United States, France, Canada and Britain. In total there are about 15 to 16 million Jews. Hebrew was revived as an everyday spoken language in the late 1800s and 1900s."]],
sp:["Jewish communities around the world","There are about 15 to 16 million Jews in the world, nearly half in Israel and most of the rest in the United States, with communities in France, Canada, Britain, Argentina and elsewhere. Over centuries Jewish communities developed different traditions: Ashkenazi Jews of Central and Eastern Europe, Sephardi Jews descended from Spain and Portugal, and Mizrahi Jews of the Middle East and North Africa."],
g:[["Torah","The first five books of the Hebrew Bible"],["Synagogue","A Jewish house of prayer and study"],["Rabbi","A Jewish religious teacher"],["Kosher","Food that follows Jewish dietary laws"]],
f:["Hanukkah, the festival of lights, lasts eight nights.","The Hebrew alphabet has 22 letters, all consonants.","Jerusalem is holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims."]},
stoic:{s:[
["Zeno and the porch","Stoicism was founded in Athens around 300 BCE by Zeno of Citium, a merchant from Cyprus who, according to legend, turned to philosophy after a shipwreck. He taught in the Stoa Poikile, the Painted Porch, on the edge of the agora. The school took its name, Stoicism, from this porch."],
["Seneca","Seneca was a Roman statesman, playwright and Stoic philosopher who lived from about 4 BCE to 65 CE. He was tutor and adviser to the emperor Nero. His Letters to Lucilius offer practical advice on friendship, time, anger and death. Accused of plotting against Nero, he was ordered to take his own life."],
["Epictetus","Epictetus was born a slave around 50 CE in what is now Turkey. After gaining his freedom, he taught philosophy in Rome and later in Greece. His teachings were written down by his student Arrian. He taught that we cannot control events, only our judgments about them and our responses."],
["Marcus Aurelius","Marcus Aurelius was Roman emperor from 161 to 180 CE. While fighting wars on the empire’s borders and dealing with a terrible plague, he wrote private notes to himself in Greek, now called the Meditations. They remind him to be patient, humble, just and to accept what cannot be changed. It is one of the best-loved books of philosophy."],
["Emotions","Stoics did not mean being emotionless, as the modern word stoic suggests. They taught that destructive emotions such as anger, fear and envy come from mistaken judgments and can be changed by reasoning. They aimed for tranquility and good feelings such as joy, kindness and caution."],
["Stoicism today","Stoicism has become popular again in the 21st century. Cognitive behavioral therapy, a widely used treatment for anxiety and depression, was inspired partly by Stoic ideas, especially Epictetus. Athletes, soldiers, business leaders and students use Stoic exercises to cope with stress and setbacks."]],
sp:["The Serenity Prayer","The popular Serenity Prayer, attributed to American theologian Reinhold Niebuhr in the 1930s and 1940s, echoes Stoic teaching: grant me the serenity to accept the things I cannot change, courage to change the things I can, and wisdom to know the difference. Epictetus had begun his Handbook nearly 1,900 years earlier with a similar thought: some things are within our power, and some are not. Recognizing the difference was, for the Stoics, the beginning of wisdom."],
g:[["Stoa","A covered porch or walkway in ancient Greece"],["Virtue","Moral excellence, the only true good for Stoics"],["Tranquility","Calmness of mind"],["Meditations","Marcus Aurelius’s private reflections"]],
f:["Memento mori, remember you will die, is a Stoic reminder to value life.","Marcus Aurelius is sometimes called the last of the five good emperors.","Stoics believed all humans are citizens of one world, cosmopolitans."]},
tao:{s:[
["The Tao Te Ching","The Tao Te Ching, the Book of the Way and Its Virtue, is a short work of 81 brief chapters, traditionally credited to Laozi. Written in poetic, often puzzling lines, it begins: The Tao that can be told is not the eternal Tao. It is one of the most translated books in the world."],
["Wu wei","A key idea of Taoism is wu wei, often translated as non-action or effortless action. It does not mean doing nothing but acting naturally, without forcing, like water flowing around rocks. Taoists believe that the softest things, like water, can overcome the hardest over time."],
["The butterfly dream","The philosopher Zhuangzi, who lived in the 300s BCE, wrote humorous, imaginative stories. In one, he dreams he is a butterfly, fluttering happily. When he wakes, he wonders: was he Zhuangzi dreaming he was a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming he was Zhuangzi? The story questions how sure we can be of reality."],
["Temples and priests","Religious Taoism developed from the 100s CE, with priests, temples, rituals and many deities, including the Jade Emperor and immortals such as the Eight Immortals. Taoist priests perform ceremonies for festivals, funerals and blessings. Famous Taoist mountains include Wudang and Mount Tai."],
["Health and long life","Taoists sought health and long life through diet, breathing exercises, meditation and movement. Practices such as tai chi and qigong are linked to Taoist ideas of balance and the flow of qi, or life energy. Traditional Chinese medicine also uses ideas of yin, yang and qi."],
["Taoism and the Chinese world","Taoism, Confucianism and Buddhism, the Three Teachings, have mixed together in Chinese culture for centuries. Many Chinese people follow elements of all three. Temples in Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore and Chinese communities around the world often blend Taoist and Buddhist traditions."]],
sp:["The uncarved block","Laozi often used the image of pu, the uncarved block, a piece of wood in its natural state before it is shaped. It represents simplicity and the potential of things before people impose rules and ambitions on them. Taoists believed people should return to this simple, natural state, with fewer desires. The idea inspired a popular 1982 book, The Tao of Pooh, which explains Taoism through Winnie-the-Pooh."],
g:[["Tao","The Way, the natural order of the universe"],["Wu wei","Effortless, natural action"],["Qi","Life energy in Chinese thought"],["Immortal","In Taoism, a person who has achieved eternal life"]],
f:["The yin-yang symbol is called the taijitu.","Legend says Laozi wrote the Tao Te Ching at a border gate before vanishing westward.","Mount Wudang in China is a famous center of Taoism and Taoist martial arts."]}
});

