// In-depth material (Animals and Nature, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
octopus:{s:[
["Mollusks with brains","Octopuses are mollusks, related to snails and clams, in a group called cephalopods, meaning head-foot, which also includes squid and cuttlefish. Over millions of years, octopuses lost their shells. There are about 300 species, from the tiny star-sucker pygmy octopus to the giant Pacific octopus."],
["Three hearts and blue blood","An octopus has three hearts. Two pump blood through the gills, and one pumps it around the body. Its blood is blue because it uses a copper-based protein, hemocyanin, rather than iron-based hemoglobin, to carry oxygen. This works well in cold water with little oxygen."],
["Ink and jets","When threatened, many octopuses squirt a cloud of dark ink to confuse predators and dull their sense of smell. They can escape quickly by jet propulsion, shooting water out through a tube called a siphon. They can also squeeze through any gap larger than their beak, the only hard part of their body."],
["Using tools","Octopuses are among the few invertebrates known to use tools. The veined octopus of the western Pacific collects coconut shell halves and carries them along the seafloor, then assembles them into a shelter. In aquariums, octopuses have learned to open jars and have escaped from tanks."],
["Changing color","Octopuses can change color and texture in a fraction of a second. Their skin contains thousands of cells called chromatophores filled with pigment, which muscles expand or shrink. Other cells reflect light. Strangely, octopuses appear to be color-blind, and scientists are still studying how they match their surroundings."],
["Mothers that guard","A female octopus lays thousands of eggs in a den and guards them, cleaning them and blowing water over them, without eating. When the eggs hatch, she usually dies. A deep-sea octopus was observed guarding her eggs for about four and a half years, the longest known brooding period of any animal."]],
sp:["The mimic octopus","The mimic octopus, discovered off Sulawesi, Indonesia, in 1998, lives in shallow waters of Southeast Asia and can imitate other animals to scare off predators. It may flatten itself and glide like a poisonous flatfish, raise striped arms like a venomous lionfish, or bury itself with two arms waving like a sea snake. Scientists think it chooses which animal to copy depending on which predator threatens it."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A mollusk with arms on its head, such as an octopus"],["Chromatophore","A color-changing skin cell"],["Siphon","A tube used to squirt water"],["Invertebrate","An animal without a backbone"]],
f:["About two-thirds of an octopus’s neurons are in its arms.","Each sucker can taste what it touches.","The plural of octopus can be octopuses or octopodes."]},
wolf:{s:[
["Gray wolves and others","The gray wolf is the largest wild member of the dog family, with males weighing about 40 to 60 kg. It once lived across most of the Northern Hemisphere. Other wolves include the red wolf of the southeastern United States, one of the most endangered canids, and the Ethiopian wolf of Africa’s highlands."],
["Hunting together","Wolves hunt large animals such as deer, elk, moose and bison by working together. They chase prey over long distances, testing herds to find weak, young or old animals. A wolf can eat about 9 kg of meat at once. Most hunts fail, so wolves can go days without food."],
["Howling","Wolves howl to communicate over long distances, up to about 10 km in open country. Howling helps gather the pack, warn rival packs to keep away and keep in touch while traveling. Wolves do not howl at the Moon; they simply point their heads up so the sound carries farther."],
["From wolf to dog","Dogs are descended from ancient wolves, probably domesticated more than 15,000 years ago, possibly when wolves scavenged near human camps. Over time, friendlier animals were bred, and today there are hundreds of dog breeds, as well as countless free-roaming village dogs around the world."],
["Yellowstone’s wolves","Wolves were wiped out from Yellowstone National Park in the United States by the 1920s. Without them, elk multiplied and ate young trees along rivers. In 1995 and 1996, 31 wolves from Canada were released there. The elk changed their behavior, trees grew back, and beavers and songbirds returned, showing how one species can shape an ecosystem."],
["Fear and folklore","Wolves appear in many stories, often as villains, as in Little Red Riding Hood and The Three Little Pigs. In other traditions they are respected: Rome’s founders, Romulus and Remus, were said to be raised by a she-wolf, and many Native American peoples honor the wolf. Attacks on people are very rare."]],
sp:["The wolf’s comeback in Europe","Wolves were hunted to extinction in much of Western Europe by the early 1900s. With legal protection and growing forests, they have returned naturally from refuges in Italy, Spain and Eastern Europe. Wolves now live in every mainland country of the European Union, and there are estimated to be around 20,000 in Europe. Their return has caused conflict with farmers over attacks on sheep, and countries are debating how strictly to protect them."],
g:[["Pack","A family group of wolves"],["Canid","A member of the dog family"],["Domestication","Taming and breeding animals for human use"],["Keystone species","A species with a large effect on its ecosystem"]],
f:["Wolf packs usually have about 4 to 9 members.","Wolves can run at about 60 km/h in short bursts.","Wolf pups are born deaf and blind."]},
frog:{s:[
["What amphibians are","Amphibians are cold-blooded vertebrates that usually begin life in water and later live partly on land. The word amphibian means double life. There are three groups: frogs and toads; salamanders and newts; and caecilians, legless burrowers that look like worms. There are over 8,000 known species."],
["Breathing through skin","Many amphibians breathe partly through their thin, moist skin, which must stay damp. Some salamanders have no lungs at all. Because their skin absorbs water and chemicals easily, amphibians are very sensitive to pollution, making them useful indicators of a healthy environment."],
["Calls","Male frogs call to attract females and warn rivals, using vocal sacs that swell like balloons to make their calls louder. Each species has its own call. On rainy nights in the tropics, the croaking of frogs and toads fills forests and rice fields, and scientists can identify species by sound alone."],
["Unusual parents","Amphibians care for their young in surprising ways. Male Darwin’s frogs carry tadpoles in their vocal sacs. Female Surinam toads carry eggs embedded in the skin of their backs. Poison dart frogs carry tadpoles on their backs to tiny pools in plants. Some frogs lay eggs on land that hatch directly into tiny froglets."],
["Chytrid fungus","Since the 1980s, a disease caused by the chytrid fungus has spread around the world, killing amphibians by damaging their skin. It has caused the decline of hundreds of species and the extinction of dozens, the worst disease-caused loss of biodiversity ever recorded. Scientists are breeding threatened species in captivity to save them."],
["The cane toad","The cane toad, native to the Americas, was introduced to many places in the 1930s and after, including Australia, the Caribbean and Pacific islands, to eat pests in sugarcane fields. It spread widely and is poisonous to animals that eat it. In Australia it has become a serious invasive species."]],
sp:["Frogs without tadpoles","Many frogs skip the tadpole stage altogether. They lay eggs on land, in moss, leaf litter or tree holes, and tiny froglets hatch straight out of them. This is called direct development. The coquí frogs of Puerto Rico, the Platymantis frogs of Southeast Asia and the rain frogs of the Americas all breed this way, which frees them from needing ponds or streams."],
g:[["Amphibian","An animal that lives both in water and on land"],["Tadpole","The water-living young of a frog"],["Vocal sac","A stretchy pouch used to amplify calls"],["Invasive species","A species that spreads harmfully in a new place"]],
f:["The Goliath frog of West Africa can weigh over 3 kg.","Frogs swallow with help from their eyes, which push food down.","Some frogs can freeze solid in winter and thaw in spring."]},
tiger:{s:[
["Where tigers live","Tigers once roamed from Turkey to eastern Russia and down to Indonesia. Today they survive in only a few percent of their former range, in India, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Russia, China and parts of Southeast Asia. They live in tropical forests, mangrove swamps, grasslands and snowy forests."],
["Bengal and Siberian tigers","The Bengal tiger of India is the most numerous. The Siberian, or Amur, tiger of the Russian Far East is the largest, with males weighing up to about 300 kg and thick fur for temperatures of −40°C. Sumatran tigers are the smallest surviving tigers. Scientists now group tigers into two subspecies: mainland and Sunda island tigers."],
["Solitary hunters","Tigers usually live and hunt alone, defending large territories marked with scent and scratches. They ambush deer, wild pigs and buffalo, stalking close before a final rush. A tiger can eat about 40 kg of meat in one meal. Most hunts fail, so tigers may hunt only every few days."],
["Swimmers","Unlike most cats, tigers love water. They swim well and often cool off in rivers and pools. In the Sundarbans mangrove forest of India and Bangladesh, tigers swim between islands and hunt fish and crabs as well as deer. Sundarbans tigers have a fearsome reputation for attacking people."],
["Cubs","Tigresses usually give birth to two to four cubs, which are born blind and helpless. The mother raises them alone, hiding them in dens and teaching them to hunt. Cubs stay with their mother for about two years before leaving to find their own territory. Many cubs die young."],
["Tigers in culture","Tigers are national animals of India, Bangladesh, Malaysia and South Korea. In Chinese tradition, the tiger is one of the 12 animals of the zodiac and a symbol of courage. Tigers appear in the folk tales of many Asian peoples, and the tiger in Rudyard Kipling’s The Jungle Book, Shere Khan, is one of the most famous in literature."]],
sp:["Counting tigers","In 2010 the 13 tiger range countries set a goal to double the number of wild tigers by 2022, the Chinese Year of the Tiger. The global count rose from about 3,200 to around 5,500 by 2023. India, home to about three-quarters of the world’s wild tigers, counts them using thousands of camera traps that photograph passing animals; each tiger can be identified by its unique stripes. Numbers have fallen, however, in parts of Southeast Asia."],
g:[["Territory","An area an animal defends"],["Camera trap","A camera triggered by passing animals"],["Subspecies","A distinct population within a species"],["Poaching","Illegal hunting"]],
f:["No two tigers have the same stripe pattern.","A tiger’s roar can be heard up to about 3 km away.","Tigers have stripes on their skin as well as their fur."]},
bat:{s:[
["More than 1,400 species","Bats make up about one-fifth of all mammal species, with more than 1,400 known. They live on every continent except Antarctica. The smallest, the bumblebee bat of Thailand and Myanmar, weighs about 2 grams. The largest fruit bats have wingspans of about 1.5 meters."],
["Fruit bats and insect bats","Most bats are small insect-eaters that use echolocation. Fruit bats, also called flying foxes, have large eyes and good noses, eat fruit and nectar and mostly do not echolocate. Some bats eat fish, frogs or even other bats. Three species feed on blood."],
["Seed spreaders and pollinators","Fruit bats spread seeds as they fly, helping forests regrow. Some bats pollinate plants, including durian, bananas, and the agave used to make tequila. In Southeast Asia, durian flowers open at night and are pollinated mainly by bats."],
["Pest control","A single insect-eating bat can eat hundreds or even thousands of insects in a night. Bats eat mosquitoes, moths and beetles that damage crops, saving farmers billions of dollars in pesticide costs worldwide. Bat droppings, called guano, are collected from caves as fertilizer."],
["Hibernation and migration","In cold places, many bats hibernate through winter in caves, lowering their body temperature and heart rate to save energy. Others migrate long distances. In the tropics, where it is warm all year, bats stay active throughout the year, though some move between roosts with the seasons."],
["Threats to bats","Bats face threats from habitat loss, hunting for food, disturbance of caves and wind turbines. In North America, a fungal disease called white-nose syndrome has killed millions of hibernating bats since 2006. Flying foxes are hunted in parts of Asia and the Pacific, and several species are endangered."]],
sp:["Giant flying foxes","The largest bats are the flying foxes of Asia, Australia and the Pacific islands. The giant golden-crowned flying fox of the Philippines and the large flying fox of Southeast Asia have wingspans of up to about 1.5 to 1.7 meters. They feed on fruit, nectar and leaves and roost in large colonies in tall trees, flying out at dusk in dramatic streams. Hunting and forest loss have made many species endangered."],
g:[["Echolocation","Finding objects by listening to echoes"],["Flying fox","A large fruit-eating bat"],["Guano","Bat or bird droppings used as fertilizer"],["Roost","A place where bats rest"]],
f:["Bracken Cave in Texas is home to about 15 million bats, the largest bat colony on Earth.","Bats are the only mammals capable of true flight.","Some bats live more than 40 years."]}
});

;
