// In-depth material (Economics and Society, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
supdem:{s:[
["Scarcity","Economics begins with scarcity: people want more than there is. Land, time, money and materials are limited, so people, businesses and governments must make choices. Every choice has an opportunity cost, the value of the next best thing given up. Supply and demand help explain how markets allocate scarce goods."],
["Equilibrium","The point where the supply curve and demand curve cross is called equilibrium. At that price, the amount buyers want equals the amount sellers offer. If the price is higher, unsold goods pile up and sellers cut prices. If it is lower, shortages appear and prices rise. Markets tend to move toward equilibrium."],
["Prices as signals","Prices carry information. When the price of something rises, it tells producers to make more and consumers to use less or find substitutes. Economist Friedrich Hayek argued that prices coordinate the knowledge of millions of people that no single planner could gather."],
["Substitutes and complements","Substitutes are goods that can replace each other, such as rice and bread or trains and buses. If the price of one rises, demand for the other increases. Complements are used together, such as cars and gasoline or printers and ink. If gas prices rise sharply, demand for cars that use a lot of it may fall."],
["Price controls","Governments sometimes set price ceilings, maximum prices, to protect consumers, or price floors, minimum prices, to protect producers. Rent controls and minimum wages are common examples, and some governments freeze prices of basic goods during emergencies. Ceilings set below equilibrium can cause shortages, and floors above it can cause surpluses."],
["Onions in 2022–2023","In the 1970s, oil producers cut supply, and the price of oil quadrupled in a few months, causing long lines at gas stations in the United States and Europe. Food prices can swing in the same way: when bad weather ruins a harvest, prices of staples such as onions, coffee or cocoa can soar until new supplies arrive."]],
sp:["The diamond-water paradox","Water is essential to life but cheap; diamonds are useless for survival but expensive. Adam Smith puzzled over this paradox in 1776. Later economists explained it with marginal utility: what matters is the value of one more unit. Because water is usually plentiful, an extra glass is worth little, while diamonds are rare, so each one is valued highly. In a desert, however, a glass of water might be worth more than a diamond."],
g:[["Scarcity","Limited resources compared with wants"],["Equilibrium","The price where supply equals demand"],["Opportunity cost","The value of the next best choice given up"],["Price ceiling","A legal maximum price"]],
f:["Concert and plane tickets often use dynamic pricing that changes with demand.","A good whose demand rises as prices rise, such as luxury goods, is sometimes called a Veblen good.","Alfred Marshall popularized supply and demand diagrams in 1890."]},
money:{s:[
["Barter","Before money, people traded goods directly, a system called barter. A farmer might trade rice for a fisher’s catch. But barter requires a double coincidence of wants: each person must want what the other has. Money solves this problem by being accepted by everyone."],
["Commodity money","Many things have served as money: cattle, salt, shells, beads, cacao beans and metals. Cowrie shells were used in Africa and Asia for centuries. The Pacific island of Yap used huge stone discs, some taller than a person, which stayed in place while ownership changed hands."],
["Paper money","Paper money was first used widely in China during the Song dynasty, around 1000 CE. Marco Polo was amazed that the emperor’s paper was accepted as payment. In Europe, banks began issuing paper notes in the 1600s. Today’s money is fiat money, valuable because governments declare it legal tender and people trust it."],
["Interest","Banks pay interest to savers and charge interest to borrowers. Interest is the price of using money over time. Simple interest is paid only on the original amount; compound interest is paid on interest as well, so savings grow faster over long periods. High interest on debts can make them hard to repay."],
["The Bangko Sentral","Most countries have a central bank, which issues the currency, sets key interest rates to control inflation, supervises banks and manages foreign reserves. The Bank of England, founded in 1694, is one of the oldest, the US Federal Reserve was created in 1913, and the European Central Bank has managed the euro since 1998."],
["Going cashless","Mobile and card payments are replacing cash in many places. In Sweden, fewer than one in ten purchases are paid in cash, China’s Alipay and WeChat Pay are used by more than a billion people, and India’s UPI handles billions of instant payments a month. Digital payments became especially popular during the COVID-19 pandemic."]],
sp:["Rai stones of Yap","On the Pacific island of Yap, people used huge stone discs called rai as money for important exchanges such as marriages and land deals. The stones, some up to about 4 meters across, were carved from limestone on Palau and carried hundreds of kilometers by canoe. Because they were too heavy to move, ownership simply passed by agreement, with everyone remembering who owned which stone, an early example of money as a shared record."],
g:[["Barter","Trading goods directly without money"],["Fiat money","Money valuable because a government declares it so"],["Interest","Payment for borrowing or lending money"],["Legal tender","Money that must be accepted for debts"]],
f:["The euro is shared by 21 European Union countries.","The word salary may come from salt, once valuable to Romans.","Most money today exists as digital records, not cash."]},
trade:{s:[
["Comparative advantage","In 1817 economist David Ricardo explained comparative advantage: even if one country is better at making everything, both countries gain if each focuses on what it gives up least to produce, and they trade. This is why countries specialize, Taiwan in chips and Bangladesh in clothing, for example, and trade for other goods."],
["The Silk Road","For centuries, the Silk Road network linked China with Central Asia, the Middle East and Europe. Traders carried silk, spices, paper, glass and horses. Ideas, religions, technologies and diseases traveled along these routes too. Sea routes across the Indian Ocean also carried huge amounts of trade."],
["The galleon trade","From 1565 to 1815, Spanish galleons sailed between Manila and Acapulco in Mexico. They carried Chinese silk and porcelain, Asian spices and other goods to the Americas and returned with Mexican silver. Manila became one of the first global trading hubs, and Spanish silver coins circulated as far as China, an early sign of a truly global economy."],
["The World Trade Organization","After the Second World War, countries created the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade, GATT, in 1947 to lower trade barriers. In 1995 it was replaced by the World Trade Organization, which sets trade rules and settles disputes between its 166 members."],
["Remittances and services","Services are a growing share of trade. India exports software and IT services worth hundreds of billions of dollars, and call centers and back offices in countries such as India and the Philippines serve customers worldwide. Migrant workers send home more than 650 billion US dollars a year in remittances, a lifeline for many families."],
["Supply chains","Modern products often have parts made in many countries. A smartphone might be designed in one country, use chips from another and be assembled in a third. These global supply chains make goods cheaper but can break down, as during the COVID-19 pandemic, when factory closures and shipping delays caused shortages worldwide."]],
sp:["The container box","In 1956 the American trucking businessman Malcom McLean loaded 58 metal containers onto a ship in New Jersey. Standard steel boxes that could move straight from ship to truck to train cut the cost of loading cargo by more than 90 percent. Today tens of millions of containers cross the oceans each year, and cheap shipping made it possible for goods to be made in one country and sold in another."],
g:[["Export","A good sold to another country"],["Import","A good bought from another country"],["Tariff","A tax on imported goods"],["Comparative advantage","The ability to produce at lower opportunity cost"]],
f:["About 80% of world trade by volume travels by sea.","About 80 percent of world trade by volume travels by sea.","The Manila–Acapulco galleon route ran for 250 years."]},
inflation:{s:[
["Prices over time","Inflation is a general rise in prices over time, which means each unit of money buys less. If inflation is 4% a year, something that costs 100 this year will cost about 104 next year. Over many years, even low inflation adds up: at 4%, prices double in about 18 years."],
["Demand-pull and cost-push","Demand-pull inflation happens when people and businesses want to buy more than the economy can produce, pulling prices up. Cost-push inflation happens when costs rise, such as oil, wages or food after a typhoon, and businesses pass them on. Printing too much money can also cause inflation."],
["The consumer price index","Statistics offices measure inflation using the consumer price index, CPI. It tracks the prices of a basket of goods and services that typical households buy, such as food, rent, electricity, transport and school fees. In poorer countries, food makes up a larger share of the basket."],
["Central banks fight inflation","When inflation is too high, central banks raise interest rates. This makes borrowing more expensive, so people and businesses spend less, cooling demand. In 1979 to 1981, the US Federal Reserve under Paul Volcker raised rates to nearly 20% to break double-digit inflation."],
["Hyperinflation","Hyperinflation is extremely rapid inflation. In Germany in 1923, prices doubled every few days, and people carried money in wheelbarrows. In Zimbabwe in 2008, a 100 trillion dollar note was printed. In Venezuela in the late 2010s, inflation reached over a million percent. Hyperinflation can destroy savings."],
["Mickey Mouse money","Hyperinflation is extremely fast inflation. In Germany in 1923, prices doubled every few days, and people carried wages home in wheelbarrows. Hungary in 1946 had the worst hyperinflation ever recorded, with prices doubling about every 15 hours, and Zimbabwe printed a 100 trillion dollar note in 2009. During wartime occupations, such as Japan’s in Southeast Asia, occupation money also became nearly worthless."]],
sp:["The 2022 inflation surge","After the COVID-19 pandemic, inflation rose around the world. Supply chains were disrupted, demand recovered quickly, and Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 pushed up oil and food prices. Inflation reached about 9% in the United States and over 10% in the euro area and Britain in 2022, the highest in 40 years. Central banks raised interest rates sharply, and inflation fell back over the following two years."],
g:[["Inflation","A general rise in prices"],["CPI","Consumer price index, a measure of prices"],["Purchasing power","How much money can buy"],["Hyperinflation","Extremely rapid inflation"]],
f:["Hungary’s hyperinflation of 1946 is the worst ever recorded.","Some countries have experienced deflation, falling prices, for years.","The Rule of 72 estimates how long prices take to double: divide 72 by the inflation rate."]},
gdp:{s:[
["What GDP counts","Gross domestic product, GDP, is the total value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a year. It counts things such as cars, rice, haircuts and phone services, but not unpaid household work or most informal trade. It is the most widely used measure of the size of an economy."],
["Simon Kuznets","GDP-like measurements were developed in the 1930s, during the Great Depression, when governments needed to know how badly the economy had shrunk. Economist Simon Kuznets created the first national income accounts for the United States. He warned that such measures do not show the welfare of a nation."],
["GDP per person","Dividing GDP by population gives GDP per capita, a rough measure of average living standards. Small, rich countries such as Luxembourg and Singapore have very high GDP per capita. Large countries such as China and India have big total GDPs but lower GDP per capita."],
["The fastest growers","Some economies have grown astonishingly fast. China grew by about 10% a year for three decades after 1980, lifting hundreds of millions out of poverty, and South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore were called Asian tigers for their rapid growth from the 1960s. In most rich countries, services make up about 70% of the economy, industry about 25% and agriculture only a few percent."],
["Purchasing power","Comparing GDP between countries is tricky because prices differ. A meal in Hanoi costs less than in Tokyo. Economists use purchasing power parity, PPP, to adjust for price differences. Measured by PPP, China has the world’s largest economy; measured in US dollars, the United States does."],
["Happiness and well-being","GDP does not measure happiness, health, inequality or the environment. Bhutan famously measures gross national happiness. The UN’s Human Development Index combines income, education and life expectancy. The World Happiness Report ranks countries by how people rate their lives."]],
sp:["The broken window","Imagine a boy breaks a shop window. The shopkeeper pays a glazier to replace it, and GDP rises. But is the town better off? French economist Frédéric Bastiat used this parable in 1850 to show the hidden cost: the shopkeeper would have spent that money on something else, like new shoes. Rebuilding after typhoons may boost GDP, but it does not mean disasters are good. GDP measures activity, not well-being."],
g:[["GDP","The total value of goods and services produced in a year"],["Per capita","Per person"],["Recession","A period when the economy shrinks"],["PPP","Purchasing power parity, adjusting for price differences"]],
f:["The world’s total GDP is over 100 trillion US dollars.","Income earned by citizens working abroad counts toward gross national income, GNI, not GDP.","Most countries publish GDP figures every quarter."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-eco2.js */
// In-depth material (Economics and Society, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
stockm:{s:[
["Why companies sell shares","Companies sell shares to raise money to grow, build factories or develop products without borrowing. The first time a company sells shares to the public is called an initial public offering, IPO. Shareholders become part-owners and may share in profits through dividends."],
["Amsterdam 1602","The Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, was the first company to sell shares to the general public that could be freely traded. Investors bought and sold them in Amsterdam, which became home to the first modern stock exchange. The company traded in spices from Southeast Asia."],
["Wall Street","The New York Stock Exchange traces its origins to 1792, when 24 brokers signed the Buttonwood Agreement under a buttonwood tree on Wall Street. It is now the world’s largest stock exchange by the value of companies listed. The Nasdaq, founded in 1971, was the first electronic stock market."],
["Dividends and prices","Investors can earn money in two ways: dividends, payments from company profits, and capital gains, when share prices rise. Prices change with company performance, interest rates, news and investors’ hopes and fears. Prices can also fall, and investors can lose money."],
["The 1929 crash","In October 1929 the US stock market crashed after a long boom fueled by borrowing. Prices fell by about 90% from 1929 to 1932. The crash helped trigger the Great Depression, a worldwide economic slump. New rules were introduced afterward to make markets safer and more honest."],
["Investing wisely","Experts advise investors to diversify, spreading money across many companies and types of investment, to reduce risk. Index funds, which track a whole market index, are a low-cost way to do this. Investing for the long term usually works better than trying to guess short-term ups and downs."]],
sp:["Tulip mania","In the 1630s, people in the Netherlands paid extraordinary prices for rare tulip bulbs. At the peak in early 1637, a single bulb of a prized variety reportedly sold for more than a house in Amsterdam. Then, in February 1637, buyers suddenly vanished and prices collapsed. Historians now think the damage was smaller than once claimed, but tulip mania remains a famous symbol of a speculative bubble."],
g:[["Share","A unit of ownership in a company"],["Dividend","A payment to shareholders from profits"],["IPO","Initial public offering, a first sale of shares"],["Bubble","Prices rising far above real value"]],
f:["Nasdaq, opened in 1971, was the first electronic stock market.","Stock exchanges once used trading floors full of shouting brokers.","Black Monday, 19 October 1987, saw the Dow fall about 22% in one day."]},
insur:{s:[
["Pooling risk","Insurance works by pooling risk. Many people pay small amounts, called premiums, into a shared fund. When one of them suffers a loss, such as a fire, accident or illness, the fund pays them. No one knows who will be unlucky, but the group can predict roughly how many losses will occur."],
["Marine insurance","Insurance for sea voyages developed in Italian city-states such as Genoa in the 1300s. Merchants paid to protect cargo against shipwreck and piracy. In the late 1600s, merchants and shipowners met at Edward Lloyd’s coffee house in London, which grew into Lloyd’s of London, still a major insurance market."],
["Life insurance","Life insurance pays money to a person’s family when they die, helping replace lost income. Modern life insurance used mortality tables showing how long people of different ages are likely to live. Today, many people also buy plans to save for education, retirement and funerals."],
["Health insurance","Health insurance helps pay for medical care. Germany created the first national health insurance system in 1883 under Otto von Bismarck. Today many countries cover all their citizens through public insurance or tax-funded systems, such as Britain’s National Health Service, while in others many people rely on private insurance through their employers."],
["Social insurance","Governments run social insurance programs for workers. The United States created Social Security in 1935, and most countries now provide pensions, sickness, maternity and disability benefits funded by workers’ and employers’ contributions. Some, like Singapore, require workers to save in individual accounts for housing, health and retirement."],
["Moral hazard","Insurance companies worry about moral hazard: insured people may take more risks because they are protected. To reduce it, policies often include deductibles, amounts the insured must pay first, and exclusions. Companies also check claims carefully to prevent fraud."]],
sp:["Typhoon insurance","Disasters can wipe out farmers’ crops and homes, and many poor families have no insurance. Governments and aid groups have experimented with catastrophe bonds and parametric insurance, which pays out automatically when a measured event, such as wind speed or rainfall, crosses a set level. Caribbean countries pooled their risk in 2007, and the World Bank has issued catastrophe bonds for countries such as Mexico, Chile and the Philippines."],
g:[["Premium","The regular payment for insurance"],["Claim","A request for payment from an insurer"],["Deductible","The amount paid before insurance covers the rest"],["Actuary","An expert who calculates insurance risks"]],
f:["Lloyd’s of London has insured unusual things, such as a pianist’s hands.","Lloyd’s of London began in a coffee house in the late 1600s.","Microinsurance offers small, cheap policies to people with low incomes."]},
urban:{s:[
["Why cities grow","People move to cities for jobs, schools, hospitals and opportunities. In rural areas, farming may provide little income. Cities offer services and markets, but rapid growth can bring crowding. This movement from countryside to city is called urbanization."],
["More than half of humanity","In 1950 about 30% of the world’s people lived in cities. Around 2007 the number of city dwellers passed half of all people for the first time. By 2050 the UN expects about two-thirds of people to live in urban areas, with most growth in Asia and Africa."],
["Megacities","A megacity has more than 10 million people. There were only two, New York and Tokyo, in 1950; today there are more than 30, most of them in Asia, Africa and Latin America, including Delhi, Shanghai, São Paulo, Cairo, Lagos, Dhaka and Manila. Lagos and Kinshasa are among the fastest-growing big cities in the world."],
["Getting around","Traffic is a major challenge in big cities. Metro systems, buses, bicycles and shared minibuses carry millions every day. Bogotá’s TransMilenio and Curitiba’s bus rapid transit inspired cities worldwide, and Singapore and London charge drivers to enter busy areas, a policy called congestion pricing."],
["Planning cities","Urban planners decide where homes, businesses, roads, parks and services should go. Baron Haussmann rebuilt Paris with wide boulevards in the 1850s and 1860s, and planned capitals such as Washington, D.C., Canberra and Brasília were designed from scratch. Today planners try to make cities walkable, green and resilient to floods and heat."],
["Green cities","Cities can reduce pollution and heat by planting trees, building parks, encouraging walking and cycling and improving public transport. The urban heat island effect makes cities hotter than the countryside. Singapore, a city full of gardens and green buildings, is an example studied by planners across Asia."]],
sp:["Tokyo","Tokyo, Japan, with about 37 million people in the Greater Tokyo Area, was long counted as the world’s largest metropolitan area; a 2025 United Nations report ranked it third, after Jakarta and Dhaka. Despite its size, it is known for being clean, safe and efficient. Its trains carry millions of passengers daily and are famously punctual, with average delays measured in seconds. Tokyo was rebuilt twice in the 20th century, after the 1923 Great Kanto earthquake and after bombing in the Second World War."],
g:[["Urbanization","The growth of cities"],["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Density","The number of people per area"],["Urban heat island","A city warmer than its surroundings"]],
f:["In 1950 only two cities in the world had more than 10 million people.","Lagos, Delhi and Dhaka are among the fastest-growing megacities.","The world has about 30 or more megacities."]},
taxes:{s:[
["Why taxes exist","Taxes are payments people and businesses make to the government. They fund public services such as schools, hospitals, roads, police, the military and social programs. Without taxes, governments could not provide goods that everyone shares, called public goods."],
["Ancient taxes","Taxes are as old as civilization. Ancient Egyptians paid taxes in grain, recorded by scribes. Roman citizens paid taxes on property and sales. In medieval Europe, peasants paid in crops and labor. The famous saying that nothing is certain except death and taxes comes from Benjamin Franklin in 1789."],
["Income tax","Income tax is charged on wages, salaries and profits. Britain introduced it in 1799 to pay for wars against Napoleon, and the United States made it permanent in 1913. Rates vary widely: some Gulf countries have no personal income tax, while top rates exceed 50 percent in parts of Europe."],
["VAT","Value-added tax, VAT, is charged on the sale of goods and services at each stage of production. France introduced it in 1954, and today more than 170 countries use it. Rates vary from around 5 to 27 percent, and some goods, such as fresh food, are often exempt. VAT raises a large share of government revenue."],
["Collecting taxes","National tax agencies, such as the Internal Revenue Service in the United States and HM Revenue and Customs in Britain, collect most national taxes, while customs services collect duties on imports. Local governments collect property taxes and business fees. Most countries set a yearly deadline for filing income tax returns."],
["Fair taxes","People debate what a fair tax system is. Progressive taxes take a larger share from people with higher incomes. Regressive taxes, such as some sales taxes, take a larger share of poorer people’s incomes. Tax evasion, illegally avoiding taxes, reduces money for public services."]],
sp:["Sin taxes","Sin taxes are taxes on products considered harmful, such as tobacco, alcohol and sugary drinks. They aim to discourage use and raise money. Mexico’s tax on sugary drinks in 2014 cut purchases, and the Philippines’ Sin Tax Reform Law of 2012 raised taxes on cigarettes and alcohol and helped fund universal health care. The World Health Organization calls tobacco taxes one of the most effective ways to reduce smoking."],
g:[["Tax","A required payment to the government"],["VAT","Value-added tax on sales"],["Progressive tax","A tax taking a larger share from higher incomes"],["Revenue","Money collected by a government"]],
f:["Some countries, such as the United Arab Emirates, have long had no personal income tax.","In ancient Rome, Emperor Vespasian taxed public urine collection.","France was the first country to introduce a value-added tax, in 1954."]},
labor:{s:[
["Work through history","For most of history, people worked on farms, as hunters or as craftspeople. Many were enslaved or bound to landowners. The Industrial Revolution brought factories, where people worked for wages, often in dangerous conditions, for long hours, including young children."],
["Unions","Workers formed trade unions to bargain together for better pay, shorter hours and safer conditions. Unions organized strikes, refusing to work until demands were met. Many were suppressed at first. Poland’s Solidarity union, founded in 1980, grew into a movement that helped end communist rule."],
["The eight-hour day","Workers campaigned for eight hours of work, eight of rest and eight of leisure. In 1886, protests in Chicago, including the Haymarket affair, became the origin of International Workers’ Day on 1 May. Uruguay was among the first countries to make the eight-hour day law, in 1915, and it became the international standard in 1919."],
["Minimum wages","A minimum wage is the lowest pay employers may legally offer. New Zealand introduced the first national minimum wage in 1894, and today most countries have one, though levels vary enormously. Debates continue over raising wages to keep up with prices while protecting jobs."],
["Migrant workers","Millions of people work abroad, as nurses, domestic workers, builders, seafarers, engineers and more. Many come from India, Mexico, the Philippines, Bangladesh and Egypt, and the Gulf states depend heavily on foreign workers. Their remittances support families and economies at home, but separation is hard on families, and migrant workers can face abuse."],
["Robots and AI","Automation and artificial intelligence are changing jobs. Machines can do some tasks in factories, offices and call centers. Some jobs may disappear, while new ones appear. Experts stress training and education so workers can adapt."]],
sp:["Child labor","Child labor is work that harms children or keeps them out of school. In the 1800s, children as young as five worked in mines and factories. Laws gradually banned it in many countries. Yet the International Labour Organization estimates about 138 million children worldwide were still in child labor in 2024, most of them working on farms in Africa and Asia. 12 June is the World Day Against Child Labour."],
g:[["Wage","Payment for work"],["Union","An organization of workers"],["Strike","Refusing to work to press demands"],["Remittance","Money sent home by workers abroad"]],
f:["The International Labour Organization was founded in 1919.","New Zealand introduced the first national minimum wage, in 1894.","Some countries are experimenting with four-day work weeks."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-food1.js */
// In-depth material (Food and Agriculture, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
rice:{s:[
["Asian and African rice","There are two species of cultivated rice. Asian rice, Oryza sativa, was domesticated in China’s Yangtze valley about 9,000 years ago and is grown worldwide. African rice, Oryza glaberrima, was domesticated separately in West Africa about 3,000 years ago. Asian rice has two main types, japonica and indica."],
["Paddies and water","Most rice grows in flooded fields called paddies. The water helps control weeds and pests. Farmers transplant seedlings by hand or machine, or sow seeds directly. Rice needs a lot of water, about 2,500 liters for each kilogram of grain in some systems. Upland rice grows on dry hillsides with rain."],
["The rice plant","Rice is a grass that grows about 1 to 1.8 meters tall. Its grains form in drooping clusters called panicles. Each grain is covered by a husk. Removing the husk produces brown rice; polishing away the bran layer produces white rice, which keeps longer but loses some vitamins and fiber."],
["IRRI","The International Rice Research Institute, IRRI, was founded in 1960 in Los Baños, Laguna. In 1966 it released IR8, a short, high-yielding variety nicknamed miracle rice, which helped prevent famine in Asia during the Green Revolution. IRRI keeps the world’s largest rice gene bank, with over 130,000 types of rice."],
["Rice in culture","Rice is central to many cuisines: Chinese fried rice, Japanese sushi, Indian biryani, Persian chelow, Spanish paella, Italian risotto, West African jollof rice and Louisiana jambalaya. In many Asian languages the word for cooked rice also means a meal, and some have separate words for rice in the field, uncooked grain and cooked rice."],
["Feeding the nation","Most rice is eaten in the country where it is grown, so only about a tenth of the world’s harvest is traded. When big exporters limit sales, prices can soar, as in the 2008 rice crisis and again when India restricted exports in 2023. Farmers face typhoons, droughts, pests and rising costs."]],
sp:["Terraces of rice","In mountainous regions, farmers carved hillsides into steps to grow rice. The Honghe Hani terraces in Yunnan, China, the Jatiluwih terraces of Bali and the rice terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. They are watered by ancient systems that channel rain from forests above, and they show how communities shaped whole landscapes over generations."],
g:[["Paddy","A flooded field for growing rice"],["Palay","Unhusked rice grain"],["Panicle","A loose, branching cluster of grains"],["Staple food","A food eaten regularly as a main part of the diet"]],
f:["More than half of the world’s people eat rice as a staple.","Only about a tenth of the world’s rice is traded between countries.","China and India together grow more than half of the world’s rice."]},
agri:{s:[
["The Fertile Crescent","One of the first places where farming began, around 11,000 to 10,000 years ago, was the Fertile Crescent, an arc of land from the Nile through the Levant and Mesopotamia. Early farmers grew wheat, barley, lentils, peas and flax and herded goats, sheep, pigs and cattle."],
["Seeds and selection","Early farmers kept seeds from the best plants to sow the next year. Over generations, crops changed: wheat grains stopped falling off the stalk, and corn grew larger cobs. This process, artificial selection, gradually transformed wild plants into crops that depend on people."],
["Villages and towns","Farming allowed people to settle in one place and store food. Villages grew into towns such as Jericho and Çatalhöyük, in modern Turkey, home to thousands of people by about 7000 BCE. Surplus food meant some people could become potters, builders, priests and rulers."],
["Tools and irrigation","Farmers invented tools such as sickles, grinding stones, hoes and later ploughs pulled by oxen. In Mesopotamia and Egypt, farmers dug canals to carry river water to fields, called irrigation. Managing water required cooperation and helped give rise to governments and laws."],
["The Columbian Exchange","After 1492, crops and animals crossed between the Americas and the rest of the world. Maize, potatoes, tomatoes, chili peppers, cacao and cassava spread from the Americas; wheat, rice, sugarcane, cattle, horses and pigs went the other way. This exchange transformed diets everywhere."],
["Farming today","Modern farms use machines, fertilizers, pesticides and improved seeds to produce much more food per hectare than ever. About a quarter of the world’s workers are in agriculture. Challenges include climate change, soil loss, water shortages and the need to grow more food sustainably for a growing population."]],
sp:["Taro, bananas and the first Pacific farmers","Farming began independently in the highlands of New Guinea about 7,000 to 10,000 years ago, where people grew taro, bananas and sugarcane. Austronesian peoples, whose ancestors are thought to have passed through Taiwan and the Philippines, later carried crops such as taro, yams, bananas and breadfruit by canoe across the Pacific, along with pigs, dogs and chickens. These crops fed island communities across thousands of kilometers of ocean."],
g:[["Domestication","Taming wild plants and animals for human use"],["Irrigation","Bringing water to crops"],["Surplus","More than is needed"],["Artificial selection","Breeding chosen plants or animals"]],
f:["Dogs were domesticated before any crop.","Around 40% of Earth’s land is used for agriculture.","The plough was one of the most important farming inventions."]},
coffee:{s:[
["The legend of Kaldi","According to legend, an Ethiopian goat herder named Kaldi noticed that his goats became lively after eating red berries from a certain shrub. Coffee plants do grow wild in the forests of Ethiopia. By the 1400s, coffee was being drunk in Sufi monasteries in Yemen to help people stay awake for night prayers."],
["Coffeehouses","Coffeehouses spread across the Ottoman Empire and reached Europe in the 1600s. In London they were nicknamed penny universities, because for the price of a cup, people could hear the latest news and debates. Lloyd’s of London and the London Stock Exchange both began in coffeehouses."],
["Arabica and robusta","Most coffee comes from two species. Arabica, about 60% of world production, grows at higher altitudes and has a smoother taste. Robusta is hardier, more bitter and has more caffeine. Two rarer species, liberica and excelsa, are grown in small quantities."],
["Roasting","Green coffee beans have little flavor. Roasting at about 200°C triggers chemical reactions that create hundreds of aroma compounds and turn the beans brown. Light roasts are more acidic and fruity; dark roasts taste more bitter and smoky. Ground coffee loses flavor quickly, so it is best brewed fresh."],
["Tea’s spread","Tea came from China, where it has been drunk for thousands of years. Buddhist monks carried it to Japan, where the tea ceremony developed. Dutch and British traders brought it to Europe in the 1600s. Britain later grew tea in India and Sri Lanka. Today tea is the most widely drunk beverage after water."],
["Caffeine","Caffeine is a natural stimulant found in coffee, tea, cacao and some other plants, where it helps protect them from insects. It makes people feel more alert by blocking a chemical in the brain that signals tiredness. Too much can cause anxiety, sleeplessness and a racing heart."]],
sp:["Coffee rust","Coffee leaf rust is a fungus that covers coffee leaves with orange powder and weakens the plants. It destroyed the coffee industry of Ceylon in the 1870s and 1880s, and in the early 2010s a new outbreak across Central America cut harvests and cost farmers their jobs. Breeders are developing resistant varieties, and growers plant shade trees and prune carefully to protect their crops. Climate change is expected to make rust and other diseases a bigger problem at some altitudes."],
g:[["Caffeine","A natural stimulant in coffee and tea"],["Roasting","Heating coffee beans to develop flavor"],["Arabica","The most widely grown coffee species"],["Liberica","A rarer coffee species with large, irregular beans"]],
f:["Ethiopia is the original home of the coffee plant.","Brazil produces about a third of the world’s coffee.","Kopi luwak is coffee made from beans that have passed through a civet, an expensive and controversial product."]},
wheat:{s:[
["Wild wheat","Wheat was one of the first crops domesticated, about 10,000 years ago in the Fertile Crescent. Early forms, einkorn and emmer, had grains tightly enclosed in husks. Over thousands of years, crossing with wild grasses produced bread wheat, which has larger grains that thresh freely."],
["Milling","To make flour, wheat grains are ground. Ancient people used grinding stones. Later, mills powered by water, wind and animals turned large millstones. Modern roller mills separate the bran and germ from the starchy endosperm to make white flour. Whole-wheat flour keeps all parts of the grain."],
["How bread rises","Bread rises when yeast, a tiny fungus, feeds on sugars in the dough and gives off carbon dioxide gas. Stretchy gluten, formed when flour proteins mix with water, traps the bubbles. Baking sets the structure. Flatbreads, such as chapati and tortillas, are made without much rising."],
["Pasta and noodles","Durum wheat, a hard wheat, is used to make pasta, such as spaghetti and macaroni. Noodles made from wheat flour are eaten across Asia, from Chinese lamian to Japanese udon and ramen. In Italy, pasta is made in hundreds of shapes."],
["Major producers","Wheat is grown on more land than any other crop. The biggest producers are China, India, Russia, the United States, France and Canada. Russia and Ukraine are major exporters, so the 2022 war affected world wheat prices. Many warm-climate countries, such as Egypt and Indonesia, grow little wheat and import it in large quantities."],
["Gluten problems","Some people cannot eat gluten. In celiac disease, gluten causes the immune system to damage the small intestine. About 1% of people worldwide have it. People with celiac disease must avoid wheat, barley and rye and eat gluten-free foods instead."]],
sp:["Sourdough","Sourdough is bread raised not with packaged yeast but with a starter, a mixture of flour and water in which wild yeasts and bacteria grow together. The bacteria make lactic acid, which gives the loaf its tang and helps it keep. Bakers feed a starter regularly, and some starters are kept alive for decades. Sourdough is one of the oldest forms of leavened bread and is still baked across Europe and North America."],
g:[["Gluten","A stretchy protein in wheat dough"],["Yeast","A fungus that makes dough rise"],["Endosperm","The starchy inner part of a grain"],["Durum","A hard wheat used for pasta"]],
f:["About 20% of the calories people eat worldwide come from wheat.","Whole-wheat flour keeps the bran and germ of the grain, while white flour is made from the starchy middle.","The oldest known bread crumbs, about 14,000 years old, were found in Jordan."]},
spice:{s:[
["What spices are","Spices are dried seeds, fruits, roots, bark or flower parts of plants used to flavor food. Pepper is a berry, cinnamon is bark, cloves are flower buds, ginger and turmeric are roots, and nutmeg is a seed. Herbs, by contrast, are usually fresh or dried leaves, such as basil or oregano."],
["Pepper, the king of spices","Black pepper comes from a vine native to southwestern India. It was so valuable that it was sometimes used as currency, and rent could be paid in peppercorns. In 408 CE, the Visigoth leader Alaric demanded thousands of pounds of pepper as part of a ransom from Rome."],
["Nutmeg and cloves","Nutmeg and mace come from a tree that grew naturally only on the tiny Banda Islands in eastern Indonesia, while cloves grew only on a few islands in the Moluccas. Their rarity made them enormously valuable in Europe, where a small bag could be worth a fortune."],
["Magellan’s voyage","Spain sent Ferdinand Magellan west in 1519 to find a route to the Spice Islands. He was killed in a battle in the Philippines in 1521, but his crew sailed on. The survivors reached the Moluccas, loaded cloves and returned to Spain in 1522 in a single ship, the Victoria, completing the first circumnavigation of the globe. The cloves paid for the voyage."],
["The Dutch monopoly","In the 1600s the Dutch East India Company seized control of the spice trade. In 1621 it conquered the Banda Islands, killing or enslaving most of the inhabitants, to control nutmeg. In 1667 the Dutch traded the island of Run to England in exchange for Manhattan, then called New Amsterdam."],
["Spices in everyday cooking","Every cuisine has its signature spices: ginger, garlic and turmeric in India, star anise and Sichuan pepper in China, saffron in Spain and Iran, and cumin and coriander across the Middle East and North Africa. Spice blends such as garam masala, ras el hanout and five-spice powder combine several spices into a single flavor."]],
sp:["Saffron","Saffron, the world’s most expensive spice by weight, comes from the threadlike red stigmas of the saffron crocus flower. Each flower has only three, and they must be picked by hand. It takes about 150,000 flowers to make a kilogram of saffron. It gives a golden color and distinctive flavor to dishes such as Spanish paella, Indian biryani and Persian rice. Iran grows about 90% of the world’s saffron."],
g:[["Spice","A dried plant part used for flavor"],["Monopoly","Complete control of a trade by one group"],["Circumnavigation","Sailing all the way around the world"],["Moluccas","The Spice Islands of eastern Indonesia"]],
f:["Vanilla is the second most expensive spice after saffron.","Chili’s heat comes from a chemical called capsaicin.","Turmeric is used in curries and gives them their yellow color."]}
});

;
