// In-depth material (Sports, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
olymp:{s:[
["Pierre de Coubertin","French educator Baron Pierre de Coubertin believed sport could build character and bring nations together in peace. He founded the International Olympic Committee in 1894, and the first modern Olympic Games were held in Athens in 1896, with about 240 athletes from 14 countries, all men."],
["Women at the Games","Women first competed at the 1900 Paris Olympics, in sports such as tennis, golf and sailing. British tennis player Charlotte Cooper became the first female Olympic champion. Women’s participation grew slowly. At Paris 2024, for the first time, equal numbers of places were given to women and men."],
["The torch relay","The Olympic flame is lit at Olympia, Greece, using sunlight focused by a curved mirror, then carried by relay to the host city. The torch relay was first held for the 1936 Berlin Games. The flame burns in a cauldron throughout the Games and is put out at the closing ceremony."],
["The Paralympics","The Paralympic Games, for athletes with disabilities, grew from a competition for injured Second World War veterans organized by Dr. Ludwig Guttmann at Stoke Mandeville in England in 1948. The first Paralympics were held in Rome in 1960."],
["Boycotts and politics","The Olympics have often been affected by politics. Many countries boycotted the 1980 Moscow Games over the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan, and the Soviet Union and allies boycotted Los Angeles in 1984. At the 1972 Munich Games, terrorists killed 11 Israeli team members."],
["New sports","The Olympic program changes over time. Recent additions include surfing, skateboarding, sport climbing and 3x3 basketball at Tokyo 2020, and breaking, or breakdancing, at Paris 2024. Some old events, such as tug of war, which was an Olympic sport from 1900 to 1920, have been dropped."]],
sp:["Jesse Owens in Berlin","At the 1936 Berlin Games, which Adolf Hitler hoped would show Nazi superiority, the African American sprinter Jesse Owens won four gold medals, in the 100 m, 200 m, long jump and 4×100 m relay. His victories were a powerful rebuke to Nazi racial theories. Back in the United States he still faced segregation and discrimination. Owens’s story remains one of the best known in the history of sport."],
g:[["IOC","International Olympic Committee"],["Paralympics","Games for athletes with disabilities"],["Boycott","Refusing to take part as a protest"],["Torch relay","Carrying the Olympic flame to the host city"]],
f:["Gold medals are mostly silver, plated with about 6 grams of gold.","The first modern Olympics, in 1896, had athletes from only about 14 countries.","The Olympic motto is Faster, Higher, Stronger – Together."]},
soccer:{s:[
["The laws of the game","In 1863 representatives of English clubs met in a London tavern and formed the Football Association, writing rules that banned carrying the ball with the hands. This separated association football, or soccer, from rugby. The International Football Association Board still oversees the laws today."],
["FIFA","The Fédération Internationale de Football Association, FIFA, was founded in Paris in 1904. It has 211 member associations, more members than the United Nations. FIFA organizes the World Cup and sets international rules."],
["The first World Cup","The first FIFA World Cup was held in Uruguay in 1930, with 13 teams. Uruguay beat Argentina 4–2 in the final in Montevideo. Brazil has won the most World Cups, five. The 2022 tournament in Qatar, won by Argentina led by Lionel Messi, had one of the most exciting finals ever."],
["Positions","Each team has 11 players. The goalkeeper is the only one who may use hands, inside the penalty area. Defenders guard their goal, midfielders link defense and attack, and forwards try to score. Teams use formations, such as 4-4-2 or 4-3-3, describing how players are arranged."],
["Club football","Club competitions draw huge followings. Top leagues include England’s Premier League, Spain’s La Liga, Germany’s Bundesliga and Italy’s Serie A. The UEFA Champions League is the most prestigious club competition. Clubs such as Real Madrid, Barcelona and Manchester United are famous worldwide."],
["Women’s football","Women’s football has grown rapidly. The first FIFA Women’s World Cup was held in 1991 and won by the United States, which has won it four times. In 2023 Spain won its first title, in Australia and New Zealand, and crowds and television audiences for women’s matches have reached record levels."]],
sp:["The Miracle of Bern","The 1954 World Cup final in Bern, Switzerland, was a huge upset: West Germany, only recently readmitted to international football, beat the great Hungarian team, which had not lost in four years, by 3 goals to 2. Germans call the match the Miracle of Bern. It lifted a nation still recovering from war, and it showed that even the best team can be beaten."],
g:[["Offside","A rule against waiting behind defenders to receive the ball"],["Penalty","A free shot at goal from 11 meters"],["Formation","How players are arranged on the field"],["Hat-trick","Three goals by one player in a game"]],
f:["About 1.5 billion people watched the 2022 World Cup final.","A football match has two halves of 45 minutes each, plus stoppage time.","A football pitch is about 100 to 110 meters long."]},
bball:{s:[
["Peach baskets","In December 1891 James Naismith, a Canadian teacher at a YMCA training school in Springfield, Massachusetts, invented basketball to keep students active indoors during winter. He nailed two peach baskets to the balcony and wrote 13 rules. The first game ended 1–0. Someone had to climb a ladder to get the ball out after each basket."],
["The NBA","The National Basketball Association was formed in 1946 as the Basketball Association of America and took its current name in 1949. Stars such as Bill Russell, Wilt Chamberlain, Magic Johnson, Larry Bird, Michael Jordan, Kobe Bryant, LeBron James and Stephen Curry made it one of the most watched sports leagues in the world."],
["The Dream Team","At the 1992 Barcelona Olympics, NBA professionals were allowed to play for the first time. The US Dream Team, with Michael Jordan, Magic Johnson and Larry Bird, won every game by an average of about 44 points. It inspired young players around the world and helped make basketball a global sport."],
["Basketball spreads","YMCA teachers carried the game around the world within a few years of its invention, to China, Japan, the Philippines, France and beyond. It became an Olympic sport at the 1936 Berlin Games, where the United States beat Canada in the final, played outdoors on a muddy tennis court. FIBA, the world governing body, was founded in 1932."],
["Leagues around the world","Besides the NBA, professional leagues thrive in Europe, where the EuroLeague brings together top clubs from Spain, Greece, Türkiye and elsewhere, and in China, Australia, Japan, the Philippines and Latin America. The Basketball Africa League began in 2021 in partnership with the NBA. Women’s basketball has its own top league, the WNBA, founded in 1996."],
["Powers of the game","The United States has won most Olympic titles, but other nations have beaten it: the Soviet Union in 1972 and 1988, and Argentina in 2004. Yugoslavia and its successor Serbia, Spain, Lithuania and France are strong in Europe, and Australia, China and the Philippines in Asia and Oceania. Recent World Cups were won by Spain in 2019 and Germany in 2023."]],
sp:["The Dream Team effect","After the 1992 Olympics, when NBA stars first played for the United States, basketball boomed worldwide. Young players from Europe, Africa, Asia and Latin America began reaching the NBA, such as Dirk Nowitzki of Germany, Yao Ming of China and Manu Ginóbili of Argentina. By the 2020s more than a quarter of NBA players were born outside the United States, and several MVP awards went to international stars."],
g:[["Dribble","Bouncing the ball while moving"],["Rebound","Catching the ball after a missed shot"],["Three-pointer","A shot from beyond the three-point line"],["Free throw","An unguarded shot after a foul"]],
f:["A basketball hoop is 3.05 meters, or 10 feet, high.","The shot clock was introduced in the NBA in 1954.","Basketball has been an Olympic sport since 1936."]},
tennis:{s:[
["Lawn tennis","Modern lawn tennis was developed in England in the 1870s. Major Walter Clopton Wingfield patented a version in 1874. The first Wimbledon Championships were held in 1877 by the All England Croquet and Lawn Tennis Club. Tennis quickly spread to the United States, France and Australia."],
["Scoring","A tennis match is divided into points, games and sets. Points are counted 15, 30, 40 and game. If both players reach 40, it is called deuce, and a player must win two points in a row to win. The word love, meaning zero, may come from the French l’oeuf, egg, but its origin is uncertain."],
["The Grand Slams","The four most important tournaments are the Grand Slams: the Australian Open in January, the French Open, on red clay, in May and June, Wimbledon, on grass, in June and July, and the US Open in August and September. Winning all four in one year is called a calendar Grand Slam."],
["Great champions","Champions include Rod Laver, who won the calendar Grand Slam twice; Martina Navratilova, with nine Wimbledon singles titles; Steffi Graf, who won a Golden Slam, all four majors and Olympic gold, in 1988; Serena Williams, with 23 Grand Slam singles titles; and Novak Djokovic, Rafael Nadal and Roger Federer."],
["Billie Jean King","Billie Jean King won 39 Grand Slam titles in singles, doubles and mixed doubles. In 1973 she beat former champion Bobby Riggs in the Battle of the Sexes match, watched by about 90 million people. She led the fight for equal prize money and helped found the Women’s Tennis Association in 1973."],
["Technology and tennis","Rackets were once made of wood. In the 1970s and 1980s, metal and graphite rackets allowed players to hit faster and harder. Electronic line-calling systems such as Hawk-Eye track the ball and decide close calls. Wimbledon replaced human line judges with electronic calling in 2025."]],
sp:["Naomi Osaka","Naomi Osaka was born in Osaka, Japan, in 1997 and grew up mostly in the United States. In 2018 she beat Serena Williams to win the US Open, and she added the Australian Open in 2019 and 2021 and the US Open again in 2020. In January 2019 she became the first Asian player to be ranked number one in the world in singles. She has also spoken openly about mental health and the pressures of top-level sport."],
g:[["Deuce","A score of 40–40"],["Ace","A serve the opponent cannot touch"],["Set","A group of games in a match"],["Grand Slam","One of the four major tournaments"]],
f:["Wimbledon players must wear almost entirely white.","The fastest recorded serves exceed 260 km/h.","Strawberries and cream are a Wimbledon tradition."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sport2.js */
// In-depth material (Sports, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
athletics:{s:[
["Ancient running","Running races were the main events of the ancient Olympic Games, which began in 776 BCE. The first and for many years only event was the stadion, a sprint of about 192 meters, the length of the stadium. Later, longer races, jumping, discus and javelin throwing and the pentathlon were added."],
["Sprints and relays","Sprints include the 100 m, 200 m and 400 m. Sprinters start from blocks and must react to the starting gun. In relays, teams of four pass a baton within a marked zone. The 4 × 100 m relay is one of the most exciting events, while the 4 × 400 m often ends a championship."],
["Hurdles and steeplechase","In hurdles races, runners leap over barriers placed along the track: 110 m hurdles for men, 100 m for women and 400 m for both. The 3,000 m steeplechase includes barriers and a water jump. Kenyan runners have dominated the Olympic men’s steeplechase for decades."],
["Jumps","The four jumping events are the long jump, triple jump, high jump and pole vault. In 1968 American Dick Fosbury won Olympic gold in the high jump by going over the bar backward, head first, a style now called the Fosbury flop and used by almost every high jumper. Swedish pole vaulter Armand Duplantis has set many world records."],
["Throws","The throwing events are the shot put, discus, hammer and javelin. Shot putters push a heavy metal ball; men’s shots weigh 7.26 kg. The hammer is a metal ball on a wire with a handle, swung around before release. Throwers compete in a circle or, for javelin, on a runway."],
["Usain Bolt","Jamaican sprinter Usain Bolt is often called the fastest man ever. At the 2009 World Championships in Berlin, he set world records of 9.58 seconds in the 100 m and 19.19 seconds in the 200 m, which still stood in 2025. He won eight Olympic gold medals and was famous for his lightning bolt pose."]],
sp:["Eliud Kipchoge","Eliud Kipchoge of Kenya is widely regarded as the greatest marathon runner in history. Born in 1984, he won Olympic marathon gold at the 2016 and 2020 Games. On 12 October 2019 in Vienna he ran 1:59:40, the first person to cover 42.195 km in under two hours, although the run did not count as an official record because of its pacing arrangements. In 2022 he set an official world record of 2:01:09 in Berlin, which Kelvin Kiptum bettered in 2023."],
g:[["Track","The oval running course"],["Field events","Jumping and throwing competitions"],["Baton","A tube passed in relay races"],["Personal best","A competitor’s best ever result"]],
f:["A standard outdoor running track is 400 meters around.","The marathon distance of 42.195 km was fixed in 1921.","Runners from Kenya and Ethiopia have long dominated the world’s distance races."]},
swimming:{s:[
["Ancient swimmers","Paintings in the Cave of Swimmers in the Egyptian desert, about 10,000 years old, appear to show people swimming. Ancient Egyptians, Greeks and Romans swam for exercise and military training. In many island cultures, swimming and diving were essential everyday skills."],
["Freestyle and the crawl","In freestyle races, swimmers may use any stroke, but almost all use the front crawl, the fastest stroke. Swimmers alternate arms overhead while kicking their legs. The crawl spread in Europe in the early 1900s after swimmers copied styles seen among Pacific Islanders and Native Americans."],
["Backstroke, breaststroke and butterfly","Backstroke is swum on the back, with alternating arms. Breaststroke uses a frog-like kick and is the slowest of the four strokes. Butterfly, with both arms swung forward together and a dolphin kick, developed from breaststroke in the 1930s. The individual medley combines all four."],
["Swimming at the Olympics","Swimming has been part of every modern Olympics since 1896. Early races were held in open water, including the River Seine in Paris in 1900. Today Olympic races take place in 50-meter pools with eight or ten lanes. Marathon swimming, 10 km in open water, was added in 2008."],
["Records and technology","Swimsuits once covered the whole body; in 2008–2009, high-tech full-body polyurethane suits helped swimmers break dozens of world records. In 2010 these suits were banned. Swimmers now wear textile suits, caps and goggles. Pools are designed to reduce waves that slow swimmers."],
["Water safety","Drowning is a leading cause of death among children in many countries. Learning to swim, wearing life jackets on boats, swimming with others and supervising children near water save lives. The World Health Organization marks World Drowning Prevention Day on 25 July."]],
sp:["Johnny Weissmuller","Johnny Weissmuller, born in 1904 in what is now Romania and raised in the United States, won five Olympic gold medals in swimming, in 1924 and 1928, and set dozens of world records. He was the first person to swim 100 m freestyle in under a minute. He later became a film star, playing Tarzan in a dozen movies, and helped make swimming popular around the world."],
g:[["Stroke","A style of swimming"],["Medley","A race using all four strokes"],["Lane","A marked section of a pool for one swimmer"],["Open water","Swimming in lakes, rivers or the sea"]],
f:["Michael Phelps won 23 Olympic gold medals.","Olympic pools are at least 2 meters deep.","Butterfly became an official separate stroke in 1952."]},
cricket:{s:[
["English beginnings","Cricket probably began in southeast England in the 1500s, played by children and later adults on village greens. By the 1700s it was popular with the aristocracy, and clubs formed. The Marylebone Cricket Club, MCC, founded in 1787 at Lord’s ground in London, became the guardian of the laws."],
["Test cricket","Test matches, the longest format, last up to five days. The first Test was played between Australia and England in Melbourne in 1877. Twelve nations now play Test cricket. Teams wear white clothing and use a red ball. Despite their length, many Tests end in a draw."],
["One-day and T20","In 1971 the first One Day International was played, with each side batting for a limited number of overs. In 2003 England introduced Twenty20, T20, in which each side faces 20 overs, about three hours in total. T20 leagues such as the Indian Premier League, started in 2008, attract huge crowds and investment."],
["Bowling","Bowlers deliver the ball with a straight arm, usually bouncing it before it reaches the batter. Fast bowlers can exceed 150 km/h. Spin bowlers make the ball turn sharply after bouncing. Bowlers try to hit the wicket or get the batter caught. Each over consists of six balls."],
["Batting","Batters try to protect their wicket and score runs. Hitting the ball over the boundary on the full scores six runs, and reaching it after it bounces scores four. Batters can also run between the wickets. A score of 100 runs is a century, a great achievement."],
["The Cricket World Cup","The first men’s Cricket World Cup was held in England in 1975, won by the West Indies. Australia has won the most titles. The Women’s World Cup began even earlier, in 1973. India’s victory in 1983 and its 2011 home win made cricket even more popular there. Cricket returns to the Olympics at Los Angeles 2028."]],
sp:["Sachin Tendulkar","Sachin Tendulkar, from Mumbai, made his Test debut for India at 16 in 1989 and played for 24 years. He scored 100 international centuries and over 34,000 international runs, both records. Known as the Little Master, he was so admired in India that some fans treated him almost like a god. In 2014 he became the first sportsperson to receive the Bharat Ratna, India’s highest civilian award."],
g:[["Over","A set of six balls bowled"],["Wicket","Three stumps topped by bails"],["Century","A score of 100 runs"],["Boundary","The edge of the field"]],
f:["Cricket has an estimated 2.5 billion fans, mostly in South Asia.","A Test match can end in a draw even after five days of play.","The Ashes series between England and Australia began in 1882."]},
martial:{s:[
["Ancient fighting arts","Fighting skills have been taught for thousands of years. Wrestling scenes appear in Egyptian tombs, and boxing and pankration, a mix of boxing and wrestling, were events at the ancient Olympics. Martial arts were practiced by soldiers, monks and ordinary people for self-defense, health and discipline."],
["Karate and judo","Karate developed in Okinawa from local fighting arts and Chinese influences and spread to mainland Japan in the 1920s. Judo, meaning gentle way, was founded by Jigoro Kano in 1882 and uses throws and holds. Judo became an Olympic sport in 1964, and karate appeared at the Tokyo 2020 Olympics."],
["Taekwondo","Taekwondo is a Korean martial art known for fast, high and spinning kicks. It was developed in the 1940s and 1950s, combining traditional Korean arts and karate. It became a full Olympic sport in 2000."],
["Boxing history","Modern boxing rules, the Marquess of Queensberry rules, were published in 1867, requiring padded gloves, three-minute rounds and a ten-second count. Great boxers include Muhammad Ali, who called himself the Greatest, Joe Louis and Sugar Ray Robinson. Boxing is an Olympic sport for men and women."],
["Famous boxers","Muhammad Ali won the world heavyweight title three times and became famous for his speed, his words and his stand against the Vietnam War draft. Sugar Ray Robinson is often called the greatest pound-for-pound boxer, and Joe Louis held the heavyweight title for more than eleven years. Manny Pacquiao of the Philippines won world titles in eight weight classes."],
["Mixed martial arts","Mixed martial arts, MMA, combines striking and grappling from many styles. It became popular through events such as the UFC, first held in 1993. Brazilian jiu-jitsu, wrestling, boxing and Muay Thai are among its core styles, and major promotions today include the UFC in the United States and ONE Championship in Asia."]],
sp:["Muhammad Ali","Muhammad Ali, born Cassius Clay in Louisville, Kentucky, in 1942, won Olympic gold in 1960 and became world heavyweight champion in 1964, when he joined the Nation of Islam and changed his name. In 1967 he refused induction into the US Army during the Vietnam War and was stripped of his title and barred from boxing for more than three years. He regained the title in 1974 in the Rumble in the Jungle in Zaire, and again in 1978. He was admired worldwide for his speed, his wit and his courage outside the ring, and he lit the cauldron at the 1996 Atlanta Olympics."],
g:[["Martial art","A system of fighting skills and discipline"],["Belt","A colored band showing a student’s rank"],["Knockout","When a boxer cannot get up within ten seconds"],["Weight division","A class grouping fighters by weight"]],
f:["Muay Thai is known as the art of eight limbs.","Bruce Lee helped make kung fu famous worldwide through films.","A black belt in many arts marks advanced skill, not the end of learning."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-lang1.js */
// In-depth material (Languages, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
langevo:{s:[
["How many languages?","About 7,000 languages are spoken in the world today. Papua New Guinea alone has over 800, Indonesia over 700, Nigeria over 500 and India over 400. About half of all people speak one of just 23 or so languages."],
["Language families","Languages that come from a common ancestor form a family. Indo-European, including English, Spanish, Hindi and Russian, has the most speakers. Sino-Tibetan includes Mandarin and Tibetan. Niger-Congo, with over 1,500 languages, has the most languages. Austronesian, which includes Malay, Malagasy and Hawaiian, spreads farthest across the globe."],
["Sir William Jones","In 1786 British judge and scholar Sir William Jones, working in India, noticed striking similarities between Sanskrit, Greek and Latin, such as words for mother and father. He suggested they had sprung from some common source, which perhaps no longer exists. This idea founded the study of the Indo-European family."],
["Grammar changes too","Languages change their grammar as well as their words. Old English had many word endings, like German today; modern English relies on word order instead. Latin had no articles, but its descendants, such as Spanish and French, developed words for the, like el and le, from Latin words for that."],
["New words","New words are created all the time. Some are blends, such as smog from smoke and fog. Some are borrowed, others shortened, such as phone from telephone. Languages also mix: Spanglish mixes Spanish and English, Hinglish Hindi and English, and Taglish Tagalog and English. Dictionaries add hundreds of words each year."],
["Saving languages","About 40% of the world’s languages are endangered, often because children grow up speaking a more dominant language. When the last speaker dies, a language becomes extinct. Some languages have been brought back: Hebrew was revived as an everyday language in the 1800s and 1900s, and Welsh and Māori are taught in schools."]],
sp:["Out of Taiwan","Linguists believe the Austronesian languages began in Taiwan about 5,000 years ago. Speakers then sailed south through the Philippines to Indonesia and Malaysia, and eventually across the Pacific and Indian Oceans, reaching Madagascar near Africa, Hawaii, Easter Island and New Zealand. With about 1,200 languages, Austronesian is one of the largest language families. Words such as mata, eye, and lima, five, are shared across this vast area."],
g:[["Language family","Languages from a common ancestor"],["Dialect","A regional or social variety of a language"],["Endangered language","A language at risk of dying out"],["Proto-language","A reconstructed ancestor language"]],
f:["Linguists reconstruct ancestor languages, such as Proto-Indo-European, by comparing descendants.","Hebrew is the best-known example of a language revived for everyday use.","Some languages have only a handful of speakers left."]},
alphabet:{s:[
["Writing begins","Writing was invented independently in several places: Mesopotamia, where Sumerians made cuneiform marks on clay about 5,200 years ago, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica. Early writing systems used hundreds or thousands of signs for words and syllables and were hard to learn."],
["The first alphabet","The first alphabet was developed about 3,800 years ago by Semitic-speaking workers in Egypt and Sinai, who adapted Egyptian hieroglyphs to represent sounds. Each sign stood for a consonant. This idea, using a small set of symbols for sounds, made writing much easier to learn."],
["The Phoenicians spread it","The Phoenicians, seafaring traders from what is now Lebanon, used a 22-letter alphabet of consonants by about 1050 BCE. As they traded across the Mediterranean, their alphabet spread. It became the ancestor of the Greek, Latin, Hebrew and Arabic alphabets."],
["Adding vowels","The Greeks adapted the Phoenician alphabet around 800 BCE and added letters for vowels, creating the first true alphabet with both consonants and vowels. Phoenician aleph, an ox, and bet, a house, became Greek alpha and beta, giving us the word alphabet."],
["The Latin alphabet","The Romans adapted the alphabet from the Etruscans, who had learned it from the Greeks. The Latin alphabet spread across Europe with the Roman Empire and Christianity. Later, colonizers and missionaries spread it worldwide. It is now the most widely used writing system, including for Vietnamese, Indonesian, Swahili and Turkish."],
["Changing scripts","Some countries have switched scripts. Türkiye replaced Arabic script with the Latin alphabet in 1928, Vietnam adopted Latin letters in the early 1900s, and Kazakhstan is moving from Cyrillic to Latin. Mongolia uses Cyrillic but is reviving its traditional vertical script, and Serbian is written in both Cyrillic and Latin letters."]],
sp:["Hangul","Hangul, the Korean alphabet, was created in 1443 by King Sejong the Great and his scholars so that ordinary people, not just the educated elite who used Chinese characters, could read and write. Its letters are designed to reflect the shapes the mouth and tongue make when pronouncing sounds. Letters are grouped into syllable blocks. Korea celebrates Hangul Day on 9 October, and linguists often praise it as one of the most logical writing systems ever created."],
g:[["Alphabet","A set of letters representing sounds"],["Consonant","A speech sound made by blocking air"],["Vowel","A speech sound made with an open mouth"],["Cuneiform","Wedge-shaped writing on clay"]],
f:["The Khmer alphabet of Cambodia has the most letters of any alphabet, 74.","The newest letter in English, J, became separate from I in the 1500s.","The Rotokas alphabet of Papua New Guinea has only 12 letters."]},
english:{s:[
["The Anglo-Saxons","English began with Germanic tribes, the Angles, Saxons and Jutes, who settled in Britain from the 400s CE. Their language, Old English, looked very different from modern English. The epic poem Beowulf was written in Old English. The word English comes from the Angles."],
["Vikings and Normans","Viking settlers from Scandinavia added Norse words, such as sky, egg, knife, they and window. In 1066 the Normans from France conquered England, and French became the language of the rulers for about 300 years. Thousands of French words entered English, such as government, court, beef and pork."],
["Chaucer and Middle English","Middle English, spoken from about 1100 to 1500, mixed Old English and French. Geoffrey Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales, written in the late 1300s, is its most famous work. In the 1400s, the Great Vowel Shift changed how long vowels were pronounced, one reason English spelling seems strange."],
["Shakespeare and the King James Bible","William Shakespeare, writing around 1600, used and invented many words and phrases still common today, such as eyeball, bedroom and break the ice. The King James Bible of 1611 also shaped English. Together they set patterns of expression that people still use."],
["English goes global","Through the British Empire and later American power, films, music and the internet, English spread worldwide. About 1.5 billion people speak it as a first or second language. It is the main language of international business, aviation, science and the internet."],
["Thomasites","English became an official or second language in many former British and American colonies, such as India, Nigeria, Kenya, Singapore and the Philippines. In India, for example, it serves as a link language among speakers of hundreds of other languages and is widely used in higher education, business and courts."]],
sp:["World Englishes","English is now spoken in many varieties, sometimes called World Englishes. Indians say prepone, the opposite of postpone. Singaporeans add lah to the end of sentences, Nigerians call a traffic jam a go-slow, and Filipinos say comfort room for toilet. The Oxford English Dictionary has added hundreds of words from these varieties, showing that English belongs to all who speak it."],
g:[["Old English","The English of the Anglo-Saxons"],["Loanword","A word borrowed from another language"],["Variety","A distinct form of a language"],["Lingua franca","A common language used between speakers of different languages"]],
f:["English has borrowed words from over 350 languages.","The Oxford English Dictionary contains over 500,000 words and phrases.","The most common letter in English is E."]},
spanish:{s:[
["Castile","Spanish developed from the Latin spoken in the Iberian Peninsula. It took its name Castilian, castellano, from the kingdom of Castile in central Spain. As Castile expanded during the Reconquista, its language spread across much of Spain. In 1492 Antonio de Nebrija published the first grammar of Spanish."],
["Arabic words","Muslim rulers controlled much of Spain from 711 to 1492, and thousands of Arabic words entered Spanish, especially words beginning with al-, such as almohada, pillow, azúcar, sugar, and aceite, oil. Many of these words passed into Portuguese and into the languages of Spain’s former colonies."],
["Spanish in the Americas","After 1492, Spanish spread across the Americas. It absorbed words from Indigenous languages, such as chocolate, tomate and aguacate from Nahuatl, the Aztec language. Mexico is now the country with the most Spanish speakers, over 120 million. Spanish varies from country to country in accent and vocabulary."],
["Sounds and spelling","Spanish spelling is mostly phonetic: words are generally pronounced as they are written. Its five vowels each have one main sound. The letter ñ, as in año, is distinctive. Questions and exclamations begin with upside-down marks, ¿ and ¡. Accents show which syllable to stress."],
["Spanish words around the world","Spanish has given words to many languages. English took words such as canyon, patio, mosquito, tornado and ranch. Philippine languages borrowed thousands, such as mesa, table, and kutsara, spoon, and many Filipino surnames come from Spanish. Guam’s Chamorro and Chavacano, a creole of the southern Philippines, also contain many Spanish words."],
["Chavacano","Spanish-based creoles developed where Spanish mixed with other languages. Chavacano, spoken by several hundred thousand people in the southern Philippines, has a largely Spanish vocabulary with grammar influenced by Philippine languages, and Palenquero in Colombia mixes Spanish with African languages. Ladino, or Judeo-Spanish, was carried by Jews expelled from Spain in 1492."]],
sp:["The Royal Spanish Academy","The Royal Spanish Academy, founded in Madrid in 1713, publishes the official dictionary and grammar of Spanish. Its motto is Limpia, fija y da esplendor, it cleans, fixes and gives splendor. Today it works with 22 other academies across the Americas, the Philippines and Equatorial Guinea, together keeping Spanish a single language understood by its hundreds of millions of speakers."],
g:[["Castilian","Another name for Spanish"],["Creole","A language formed from mixing languages that becomes a mother tongue"],["Phonetic","Spelled as it sounds"],["Reconquista","The Christian reconquest of Spain"]],
f:["Spanish is the world’s second most spoken native language, after Mandarin.","About 90 percent of Spanish speakers live in the Americas.","El Día del Idioma Español is celebrated on 23 April."]}
});

;
