// In-depth material (Languages, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
mandarin:{s:[
["The most spoken language","Mandarin Chinese has more native speakers than any other language, about 940 million, and over 1.1 billion speakers in total. It is the official language of China and Taiwan and one of the official languages of Singapore. It is one of the six official languages of the United Nations."],
["Putonghua","Standard Mandarin, called Putonghua, common speech, in mainland China, is based on the dialect of Beijing. Before the 20th century, people across China spoke many very different varieties, such as Cantonese, Hokkien, Shanghainese and Hakka. These are so different that speakers often cannot understand each other, though they share a writing system."],
["Pinyin","Pinyin is the official system for writing Mandarin sounds in the Latin alphabet, adopted in China in 1958. It helps children and foreigners learn pronunciation, and it is used for typing Chinese on computers and phones. Accent marks show tones, as in mā, má, mǎ and mà."],
["Simple and traditional characters","In the 1950s and 1960s, mainland China simplified thousands of characters, reducing the number of strokes to make them easier to learn. Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau still use traditional characters. For example, the character for horse is 马 in simplified and 馬 in traditional form."],
["Measure words","Mandarin uses measure words between a number and a noun, a little like saying a sheet of paper in English. Different measure words go with different kinds of things: 本 běn for books, 张 zhāng for flat things like tables and paper, and 个 gè as a general one. Learners must memorize which measure words go with which nouns."],
["Learning Mandarin","Mandarin is increasingly studied around the world. Universities and Confucius Institutes teach it in many countries, and the HSK exam measures a learner’s level. Chinese New Year, called Spring Festival, is celebrated by Chinese communities worldwide and is a public holiday in several countries."]],
sp:["Cantonese and Hokkien","Mandarin is only one of many Chinese languages. Cantonese is spoken in Guangdong, Hong Kong and Macau and by many overseas Chinese communities, and Hokkien, a Southern Min language, is spoken in Fujian, in Taiwan and across Southeast Asia. These languages sound very different from Mandarin, although all are written with the same characters. This is why standard Mandarin, taught in schools across China, matters so much for communication between regions."],
g:[["Tone","A pitch pattern that changes a word’s meaning"],["Pinyin","Mandarin written with Latin letters"],["Character","A written Chinese symbol"],["Measure word","A word used between a number and a noun"]],
f:["The character for person, 人, looks like a walking figure.","A Chinese dictionary may contain over 50,000 characters, but about 3,000 cover most daily reading.","Chinese has been written for more than 3,000 years."]},
latin:{s:[
["From Latium to an empire","Latin began as the language of the Latins, a people of the region of Latium around Rome, about 3,000 years ago. As Rome conquered Italy and then the Mediterranean world, Latin spread with soldiers, settlers and officials. In the western Roman Empire, it replaced many local languages."],
["Cases and word order","Latin is an inflected language: word endings show a word’s role in the sentence. In Puella canem videt, the girl sees the dog, and in Canem puella videt, the meaning stays the same. Changing endings changes meaning. Latin nouns have up to six cases."],
["Vulgar Latin","Ordinary people spoke Vulgar Latin, everyday Latin that differed from the polished language of writers like Cicero. After the western Roman Empire fell in 476, Vulgar Latin developed differently in different regions, eventually becoming the Romance languages: Italian, Spanish, French, Portuguese and Romanian."],
["The Church and scholars","Latin remained the language of the Catholic Church and of learning in Europe for over a thousand years. Scholars such as Thomas Aquinas, Copernicus, Galileo and Newton wrote major works in Latin. Until the 1960s, the Catholic Mass was celebrated in Latin worldwide."],
["Latin in law and medicine","Many legal and medical terms come from Latin: habeas corpus, ad hoc, alibi, affidavit and subpoena in law, and terms such as femur, cranium, vertebra and in vitro in medicine. Scientists use Latin names for species, such as Homo sapiens, wise human, and Panthera leo, the lion."],
["Mottoes","Many institutions use Latin mottoes. The University of Santo Tomas has Veritas in Caritate, truth in charity. The United States uses E pluribus unum, out of many, one. Harvard has Veritas, truth. The Olympic motto, Citius, Altius, Fortius, means faster, higher, stronger."]],
sp:["The Vatican’s Latin","Latin is still an official language of Vatican City. The Vatican publishes official documents in Latin and even has a Latin dictionary of modern words, the Lexicon Recentis Latinitatis, which created Latin terms for things like computers, instrumentum computatorium, and jeans, bracae linteae caeruleae. Popes have sent messages in Latin on social media. The Vatican had cash machines with instructions in Latin."],
g:[["Inflection","Changes in word endings to show grammar"],["Romance languages","Languages descended from Latin"],["Motto","A short phrase expressing an ideal"],["Vulgar Latin","Everyday spoken Latin"]],
f:["About 60% of English words come from Latin, directly or through French.","Carpe diem means seize the day.","The abbreviations a.m. and p.m. stand for ante meridiem and post meridiem."]},
arabic:{s:[
["Speakers","Arabic is spoken by over 400 million people across the Middle East and North Africa and is an official language in more than 20 countries. It is one of the six official languages of the United Nations. As the language of the Quran, it is also studied by many of the world’s nearly 2 billion Muslims."],
["Right to left","Arabic is written from right to left in a flowing, joined script with 28 letters. Most letters change shape depending on whether they come at the start, middle or end of a word. Short vowels are usually not written but can be shown by small marks above and below letters."],
["Classical and spoken Arabic","Modern Standard Arabic, based on the Classical Arabic of the Quran, is used in books, newspapers, news broadcasts and formal speeches across the Arab world. In daily life, people speak regional dialects, such as Egyptian, Levantine, Gulf and Maghrebi Arabic, which can differ greatly from one another."],
["Words from Arabic","English has borrowed many words from Arabic, often through Spanish or Latin: algebra, algorithm, alcohol, coffee, sugar, cotton, sofa, magazine, zero and giraffe. Many star names, such as Aldebaran and Betelgeuse, are Arabic too. Arabic numerals reached Europe through Arabic texts."],
["A language of learning","During the Islamic Golden Age, Arabic was the international language of science and philosophy. Greek, Persian and Indian works were translated into Arabic, and scholars wrote new works in it. Later, many of these Arabic books were translated into Latin, helping to spark learning in medieval Europe."],
["Arabic loanwords","Spanish has thousands of words of Arabic origin, including azúcar, almohada and aceite. English borrowed algebra, algorithm, zero, coffee, sugar and alcohol. Swahili takes its name from the Arabic word sawāḥil, meaning coasts, and Malay, Persian and Urdu all contain many Arabic words, often written in an Arabic-based script."]],
sp:["The Kufic and Naskh scripts","Arabic calligraphy developed several major styles. Kufic, one of the oldest, named after the city of Kufa in Iraq, is angular and was used for early Quran manuscripts and on buildings. Naskh, rounder and easier to read, became the standard for books and is the basis of most printed Arabic today. Thuluth is a grand decorative style used on mosques, and Diwani was used by the Ottoman court for official documents."],
g:[["Script","A system of written characters"],["Dialect","A regional form of a language"],["Calligraphy","The art of beautiful writing"],["Jawi","Arabic script adapted for Malay and related languages"]],
f:["The word Arabic in Arabic is al-ʿarabiyya.","UN Arabic Language Day is 18 December.","Arabic has many words for camel and love."]},
signlang:{s:[
["How sign languages work","Sign languages use handshapes, movements, locations and facial expressions to communicate. Raising the eyebrows can turn a statement into a question. Signers use the space around them to show who is doing what to whom. Sign languages have their own grammar, different from the spoken languages around them."],
["Many sign languages","There is no single universal sign language. There are more than 300 sign languages in the world. American Sign Language and British Sign Language are very different, though both countries speak English. Japanese Sign Language, Libras in Brazil and Auslan in Australia are other examples."],
["The first schools","In the 1700s, Charles-Michel de l’Épée founded a free school for deaf children in Paris, using signs. In 1817 Thomas Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc, a deaf teacher from the Paris school, founded the first American school for the deaf in Connecticut. Gallaudet University, for deaf students, was founded in 1864."],
["The Milan Congress","In 1880 a conference of educators in Milan voted to ban sign language in deaf schools in favor of oralism, teaching deaf children only to lip-read and speak. For decades, children were punished for signing. Many deaf people believe this harmed their education. In 2010 the organizers’ successors formally rejected the Milan resolutions."],
["Nicaraguan Sign Language","In the 1970s and 1980s, deaf children brought together in new schools in Nicaragua created their own sign language. Younger children made it more complex and regular. Linguists studied it as a rare chance to watch a new language being born, showing that children naturally create grammar."],
["Deaf education","The first public school for deaf children was founded in Paris by the Abbé de l’Épée in the 1760s. In 1817 Thomas Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc opened the American School for the Deaf in Hartford, and Gallaudet University in Washington, D.C., founded in 1864, is the world’s best-known university for deaf students. In many countries, schools for the deaf teach in sign language."]],
sp:["Deaf culture","Many deaf people see themselves as part of a cultural and linguistic community, often writing Deaf with a capital D. Deaf culture has its own traditions, humor, poetry, theater and history, shared through sign language. Deaf people get attention by tapping shoulders, waving or flicking lights. September is celebrated as Deaf Awareness Month, and 23 September is the International Day of Sign Languages, chosen because the World Federation of the Deaf was founded on that day in 1951."],
g:[["Sign language","A visual language using hands, face and body"],["Fingerspelling","Spelling words letter by letter with the hands"],["Interpreter","A person who translates between languages"],["Oralism","Teaching deaf people only through speech"]],
f:["Babies can learn to sign before they can speak.","Some animals, such as the gorilla Koko, were taught signs, though scientists debate whether this is language.","Helen Keller, who was deaf and blind, learned language through signs spelled into her hand."]}
});

;
