// In-depth material (Arts and Literature, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
shakes:{s:[
["The lost years","Shakespeare was born in Stratford-upon-Avon in 1564, the son of a glove maker. In 1582, at 18, he married Anne Hathaway, and they had three children, including twins, Hamnet and Judith. Little is known about him between 1585 and 1592, called the lost years. By 1592 he was working as an actor and writer in London."],
["The tragedies","Shakespeare’s great tragedies include Hamlet, about a prince seeking revenge for his father’s murder; Othello, destroyed by jealousy; King Lear, betrayed by his daughters; and Macbeth, driven by ambition to murder. Romeo and Juliet tells of two young lovers from feuding families whose deaths reconcile them."],
["Comedies and romances","His comedies, such as A Midsummer Night’s Dream, Twelfth Night and Much Ado About Nothing, feature mistaken identities, disguises and marriages. Later plays, such as The Tempest and The Winter’s Tale, are called romances, mixing sorrow, magic and reconciliation."],
["The sonnets","Shakespeare wrote 154 sonnets, 14-line poems, published in 1609. Many are addressed to a young man and others to a mysterious Dark Lady. Sonnet 18 begins Shall I compare thee to a summer’s day? They explore love, beauty, time and death and are among the most admired poems in English."],
["Shakespeare’s company","Shakespeare was a member and part-owner of an acting company called the Lord Chamberlain’s Men, renamed the King’s Men when James I became their patron in 1603. Female roles were played by boys, as women were not allowed on the public stage in England until 1660."],
["Shakespeare around the world","Shakespeare’s plays have been translated into more than 100 languages and are performed on every continent. Akira Kurosawa reset Macbeth in samurai Japan in Throne of Blood and King Lear in Ran, and Indian films such as Maqbool and Haider retold Macbeth and Hamlet. Films have also reset his stories in modern times, such as West Side Story, based on Romeo and Juliet, and The Lion King, which echoes Hamlet."]],
sp:["The Globe Theatre","The Globe Theatre was built in 1599 on the south bank of the River Thames in London by Shakespeare’s company. It was a round, open-air wooden playhouse holding up to about 3,000 people. Poorer spectators, called groundlings, paid a penny to stand in the yard in front of the stage. In 1613 a cannon fired during a performance of Henry VIII set the thatched roof alight, and the theater burned down. A reconstruction, Shakespeare’s Globe, opened near the original site in 1997."],
g:[["Sonnet","A 14-line poem"],["Tragedy","A play ending in disaster for the hero"],["Soliloquy","A speech in which a character thinks aloud"],["Groundling","A standing spectator at the Globe"]],
f:["Shakespeare is thought to have been born and died on or near 23 April.","He left his wife his second-best bed in his will.","Hamlet is his longest play."]},
homer:{s:[
["Bards and memory","The Iliad and the Odyssey were composed in the oral tradition, recited by bards called aoidoi, who sang to the lyre. They used repeated phrases, called epithets, such as rosy-fingered dawn and swift-footed Achilles, which helped them remember and improvise long poems. Writing them down came later."],
["Dactylic hexameter","Homer’s poems are written in dactylic hexameter, a line of six rhythmic units, each usually a long syllable followed by two short ones. The Iliad has about 15,700 lines and the Odyssey about 12,100. Reciting either in full would take many hours, spread over several evenings."],
["Achilles","The Iliad focuses on the anger of Achilles, the greatest Greek warrior, during the tenth year of the Trojan War. After a quarrel with the commander Agamemnon, Achilles refuses to fight. Only when his friend Patroclus is killed by the Trojan prince Hector does he return to battle, killing Hector in revenge."],
["Odysseus’s journey","The Odyssey follows Odysseus’s ten-year journey home to Ithaca after the war. He outwits the one-eyed Cyclops Polyphemus, resists the enchanting Sirens, escapes the witch Circe and sails between the monster Scylla and the whirlpool Charybdis. At home, his faithful wife Penelope is surrounded by suitors."],
["Gods in the poems","The gods take sides in Homer’s poems. Athena, goddess of wisdom, helps Odysseus. Poseidon, god of the sea, punishes him for blinding his son, the Cyclops. In the Iliad, Zeus weighs the fates of heroes, while Aphrodite supports Troy. The gods often behave like quarreling humans."],
["Homer’s influence","Homer’s epics were the foundation of Greek education and inspired later works such as Virgil’s Aeneid and James Joyce’s Ulysses. The word odyssey now means any long, adventurous journey. Expressions such as Achilles’ heel and Trojan horse come from the Trojan War stories."]],
sp:["Heinrich Schliemann and Troy","In the 1870s German businessman Heinrich Schliemann dug into a mound called Hisarlik in northwestern Turkey, convinced it was Troy. He found the ruins of several cities built one on top of another and a hoard of gold he called Priam’s Treasure. His methods were crude and damaged the site, and his claims were sometimes exaggerated. Later archaeologists showed that one layer, Troy VII, destroyed around 1180 BCE, may match the legendary war."],
g:[["Epic","A long poem about heroes"],["Bard","A poet who recites or sings verses"],["Epithet","A descriptive phrase attached to a name"],["Odyssey","A long, adventurous journey"]],
f:["Homer is traditionally said to have been blind.","An Achilles’ heel is a weak point, after the hero’s only vulnerable spot.","The Iliad takes its name from Ilion, another name for Troy."]},
novel:{s:[
["What makes a novel","A novel is a long work of fiction written in prose, telling a story through characters, plot and setting. Unlike epics, novels often focus on ordinary people and their inner thoughts and feelings. The word novel comes from the Italian novella, meaning a new story."],
["The Tale of Genji","Often called the world’s first novel, The Tale of Genji was written in Japan in the early 1000s by Murasaki Shikibu, a lady-in-waiting at the imperial court. It tells of the life and loves of Prince Genji in elegant detail and is still widely read and admired."],
["Don Quixote","Miguel de Cervantes’ Don Quixote, published in two parts in 1605 and 1615, is often called the first modern European novel. It tells of a man who reads so many tales of knights that he sets out as a knight himself, attacking windmills he believes are giants. It mixes comedy and sadness."],
["The great 19th-century novelists","The 1800s were a golden age for the novel. Charles Dickens wrote Oliver Twist and Great Expectations; Leo Tolstoy, War and Peace; Fyodor Dostoevsky, Crime and Punishment; Victor Hugo, Les Misérables; and Mary Shelley, Frankenstein, often called the first science-fiction novel."],
["Novels and empire","Novels have often challenged empires. José Rizal’s Noli Me Tángere, 1887, exposed abuses of Spanish rule in the Philippines and helped inspire a revolution. Chinua Achebe’s Things Fall Apart, 1958, showed the destruction of Igbo society under British rule in Nigeria, and Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o of Kenya wrote novels in his own language, Gikuyu, to resist the hold of English."],
["Reading novels today","Novels are read in print, on e-readers and as audiobooks. Popular genres include mystery, fantasy, romance and science fiction. Online platforms, where writers post stories chapter by chapter, have produced bestsellers later published as books and adapted into films and TV series."]],
sp:["Web novels","The internet has created new ways to publish novels. In China, millions of writers post serial novels on web literature sites, and the most popular are turned into TV dramas and games. In Japan, web novels often become light novels, manga and anime. Wattpad, launched in Canada in 2006, gave young writers worldwide a direct audience, and hits such as After became printed books and films."],
g:[["Novel","A long work of prose fiction"],["Plot","The sequence of events in a story"],["Protagonist","The main character"],["Genre","A category such as mystery or fantasy"]],
f:["Some of Dickens’s novels were first published in weekly or monthly parts.","War and Peace has more than 500 characters.","Things Fall Apart, 1958, by Chinua Achebe, has sold more than 20 million copies."]},
impress:{s:[
["Paris in the 1860s","In the 1860s, Paris was being rebuilt with wide boulevards, cafés, parks and railway stations. Young painters wanted to show this modern life and the changing effects of light and weather. They gathered at cafés such as the Café Guerbois to discuss new ideas about art."],
["The first exhibition","In 1874, about 30 artists, including Claude Monet, Pierre-Auguste Renoir, Edgar Degas, Camille Pissarro, Alfred Sisley, Berthe Morisot and Paul Cézanne, held an independent exhibition in the former studio of the photographer Nadar. A critic mocked Monet’s Impression, Sunrise, calling the group Impressionists. The name stuck."],
["Painting outdoors","The Impressionists often painted en plein air, outdoors, to capture fleeting effects of light. New paint in metal tubes, invented in 1841, made this easier. They used quick, visible brushstrokes and bright, unmixed colors, and avoided black, showing shadows in blues and purples."],
["Monet’s series","Claude Monet painted the same subject at different times of day and seasons to show how light changes it, as in his series of haystacks, Rouen Cathedral and the Houses of Parliament in London. In his garden at Giverny, he painted his famous water lilies around 250 times."],
["Degas and Renoir","Edgar Degas painted ballet dancers, horse races and everyday scenes, often from unusual angles, and preferred to call himself a realist. Renoir painted joyful scenes of people dancing and enjoying themselves, such as Dance at Le Moulin de la Galette. Mary Cassatt, an American, painted tender scenes of mothers and children."],
["Impressionism’s influence","At first, many critics and buyers rejected Impressionism. By the 1890s it had gained acceptance, and it opened the way for modern art. Painters around the world adapted its ideas about light, from Mary Cassatt in the United States and Joaquín Sorolla in Spain to Fernando Amorsolo in the Philippines and Kuroda Seiki in Japan."]],
sp:["Sorolla, painter of light","Joaquín Sorolla, born in Valencia, Spain, in 1863, became famous for dazzling scenes of sunlit beaches, children playing in the sea and fishermen at work. He painted quickly outdoors to catch the flicker of Mediterranean light on water and white cloth. His huge murals of the regions of Spain fill a hall in New York, and his home in Madrid is now a museum. His style influenced painters as far away as Latin America and Asia."],
g:[["Impressionism","An art style capturing light and moments"],["En plein air","Painting outdoors"],["Brushstroke","A mark left by a paintbrush"],["Salon","The official annual art exhibition in Paris"]],
f:["Monet had cataracts later in life, which affected the colors he saw.","Impression, Sunrise shows the port of Le Havre.","Berthe Morisot was one of the few women in the original group."]},
musicw:{s:[
["Gregorian chant","Medieval Western music grew in churches and monasteries. Gregorian chant, named after Pope Gregory I, is a single melody sung in Latin without accompaniment. Monks developed early music notation in the 800s and 900s to help remember the chants, the beginning of written Western music."],
["Bach and Handel","The Baroque period, from about 1600 to 1750, featured elaborate, ornamented music. Johann Sebastian Bach wrote masterpieces for church, keyboard and orchestra, such as the Brandenburg Concertos. George Frideric Handel wrote the oratorio Messiah, with its Hallelujah chorus. Antonio Vivaldi wrote The Four Seasons."],
["Haydn and Beethoven","The Classical period, about 1750 to 1820, favored clear, balanced forms such as the symphony and sonata. Joseph Haydn, called the father of the symphony, wrote 104 of them. Ludwig van Beethoven, who became deaf, wrote nine symphonies, bridging the Classical and Romantic eras. His Ninth includes the Ode to Joy."],
["The Romantic era","In the 1800s, Romantic composers expressed powerful emotions, nature and national pride. Frédéric Chopin wrote poetic piano music; Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky composed ballets such as Swan Lake and The Nutcracker; and Richard Wagner wrote enormous operas. Orchestras grew larger."],
["The 20th century","Composers in the 1900s experimented with new sounds. Claude Debussy created dreamy harmonies; Igor Stravinsky’s The Rite of Spring caused a riot at its premiere in 1913; and Arnold Schoenberg abandoned traditional keys. Film music, by composers such as John Williams, brought orchestral music to new audiences."],
["Composers beyond Europe","Many composers outside Europe have won worldwide fame. Florence Price became the first African American woman to have a symphony played by a major orchestra, in 1933. Tōru Takemitsu of Japan, Tan Dun of China, Arvo Pärt of Estonia and Lucrecia Kasilag of the Philippines drew on the sounds of their own cultures."]],
sp:["El Sistema","In 1975 the Venezuelan economist and musician José Antonio Abreu founded El Sistema, a program offering free music training to children, many from poor neighborhoods. Hundreds of thousands of young Venezuelans have learned to play in its orchestras. Its most famous graduate, the conductor Gustavo Dudamel, leads orchestras around the world, and programs inspired by El Sistema now run in dozens of countries."],
g:[["Symphony","A large work for orchestra, usually in four movements"],["Concerto","A work for a solo instrument and orchestra"],["Oratorio","A large religious work for voices and orchestra"],["Notation","Writing music with symbols"]],
f:["A standard orchestra has about 70 to 100 musicians.","Beethoven composed some of his greatest music after becoming deaf.","The piano was invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori around 1700."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-arts2.js */
// In-depth material (Arts and Literature, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
cinema:{s:[
["Moving pictures","In 1878 photographer Eadweard Muybridge used a row of cameras to photograph a galloping horse, proving that all four hooves leave the ground at once. Thomas Edison’s company developed the Kinetoscope in the early 1890s, a cabinet through which one person at a time could watch short films."],
["The Lumière brothers","On 28 December 1895, Auguste and Louis Lumière held one of the first public film screenings for a paying audience, in a Paris café, using their Cinématographe, a camera and projector in one. Their short films showed everyday scenes, such as workers leaving a factory and a train arriving at a station."],
["Georges Méliès","Georges Méliès, a French stage magician, discovered that cameras could create illusions. He made objects appear and vanish, people shrink and grow and rockets fly. His A Trip to the Moon of 1902, in which a rocket lands in the eye of the Man in the Moon, is one of the most famous early films."],
["Hollywood","In the 1910s, American filmmakers moved to Hollywood in southern California for its sunny weather and varied landscapes. Studios such as Paramount, Warner Bros. and MGM produced hundreds of films a year. The first Academy Awards, the Oscars, were presented in 1929."],
["Talkies","Early films were silent, accompanied by live music and title cards. In 1927 The Jazz Singer became the first feature film with synchronized spoken dialogue and songs, starting the era of talkies. Within a few years, silent films had almost disappeared, and some silent stars’ careers ended."],
["World cinema","Many countries have great film traditions. India’s Bollywood, based in Mumbai, makes more films than any other industry. Japan produced masters such as Akira Kurosawa, and South Korea’s Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020, the first non-English-language film to do so. Nigeria’s Nollywood is one of the largest film industries."]],
sp:["Cinema conquers the world","Within months of their first show in Paris in 1895, the Lumière brothers sent camera operators around the world. In 1896 audiences watched moving pictures in London, New York, Bombay, Buenos Aires, Saint Petersburg and many other cities, and the operators filmed scenes wherever they went, creating some of the earliest moving images of daily life in dozens of countries."],
g:[["Projector","A machine that shows films on a screen"],["Talkie","An early film with sound"],["Feature film","A full-length movie"],["Special effects","Tricks to create illusions on screen"]],
f:["Standard films are shown at 24 frames per second.","India produces more feature films than any other country.","Nora Aunor was named National Artist for Film in 2022."]},
davinci:{s:[
["Vinci","Leonardo was born on 15 April 1452 near the small town of Vinci in Tuscany, Italy, the son of a notary and a peasant woman named Caterina. Leonardo da Vinci simply means Leonardo from Vinci. Because his parents were not married, he could not attend university, so he was largely self-taught."],
["Milan","Around 1482 Leonardo moved to Milan to work for the duke, Ludovico Sforza. In a letter, he described himself mainly as a military engineer who could build bridges, cannons and armored vehicles, mentioning painting only at the end. In Milan he painted The Last Supper and designed a giant bronze horse that was never cast."],
["The Last Supper","Leonardo painted The Last Supper on a wall of the dining hall of the convent of Santa Maria delle Grazie in Milan, from about 1495 to 1498. It shows the moment Jesus says one of his disciples will betray him. Leonardo experimented with oil and tempera on dry plaster, which began to flake within decades. It has been restored many times."],
["Anatomy","Leonardo dissected about 30 human bodies and made detailed drawings of muscles, bones, the heart and a fetus in the womb. He discovered how heart valves work and studied the brain. His anatomical drawings were centuries ahead of their time but were not published until long after his death."],
["Mirror writing","Leonardo filled his notebooks, some 7,000 pages of which survive, with notes written right to left, in mirror writing, which can be read in a mirror. He may have done this because he was left-handed, which avoided smudging the ink. His notebooks cover painting, flight, water, geology, optics and machines."],
["Last years in France","In 1516 Leonardo accepted an invitation from the French king Francis I and moved to the Château du Clos Lucé near Amboise, taking the Mona Lisa with him. He spent his last years sketching, designing and talking with the king. He died on 2 May 1519 and is buried at Amboise."]],
sp:["The Vitruvian Man","The Vitruvian Man, drawn by Leonardo around 1490, shows a man with outstretched arms and legs inscribed in both a circle and a square. It illustrates the ideas of the Roman architect Vitruvius, who wrote that the human body follows ideal proportions. The drawing combines art, mathematics and anatomy and has become a symbol of the Renaissance. It is kept in Venice and rarely displayed, because light damages it, and it appears on Italy’s one-euro coin."],
g:[["Renaissance","The rebirth of art and learning in Europe"],["Fresco","Painting on wet plaster"],["Sfumato","Soft, smoky blending of tones"],["Anatomy","The study of the body’s structure"]],
f:["Fewer than 20 paintings are generally accepted as Leonardo’s.","Salvator Mundi, attributed to Leonardo, sold for about 450 million US dollars in 2017.","Leonardo designed a flying machine with flapping wings."]},
gothic:{s:[
["After the Romanesque","Before Gothic, European churches were built in the Romanesque style, with thick walls, round arches and small windows, making them dark inside. In the 1100s builders in France found ways to build higher, lighter churches. The new style later became known as Gothic, a name given by Renaissance critics who thought it barbaric."],
["Saint-Denis","The Gothic style began with the rebuilding of the choir of the Abbey of Saint-Denis near Paris, completed in 1144 under Abbot Suger. He wanted a church filled with light, which he saw as a symbol of God. The new choir used pointed arches, ribbed vaults and large stained-glass windows."],
["Pointed arches and ribbed vaults","Pointed arches direct the weight of the roof more steeply downward than round arches, allowing taller buildings. Ribbed vaults, crossing stone arches on the ceiling, carry the weight to a few points, so walls do not need to be as thick. This allowed walls to be filled with windows."],
["Flying buttresses","Flying buttresses are stone arms on the outside of a building that lean against the walls and carry the outward push of the roof down to the ground. They allowed Gothic cathedrals to reach great heights. The choir of Beauvais Cathedral in France, at about 48 meters, collapsed in 1284 and had to be rebuilt."],
["Notre-Dame","Notre-Dame de Paris was built from 1163 to about 1260, with later changes. On 15 April 2019, fire destroyed its roof and spire. Donations poured in from around the world, and after five years of restoration by hundreds of craftspeople, it reopened on 7 December 2024. Its famous rose windows survived the fire."],
["Gothic around the world","Gothic Revival spread across the world in the 1800s. St. Patrick’s Cathedral in New York opened in 1879, and the Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus railway station in Mumbai, completed in 1888, mixes Gothic with Indian styles. The San Sebastian Church in Manila, finished in 1891, is made almost entirely of steel prefabricated in Belgium, a rare Gothic church built to resist earthquakes and fires."]],
sp:["Building a cathedral","Building a Gothic cathedral often took a century or more and involved hundreds of workers: masons, carpenters, glassmakers, sculptors and laborers. Master masons designed buildings without modern engineering, using geometry and experience. Stone was carried by cart and boat and lifted with treadwheel cranes powered by men walking inside large wheels. Many builders never lived to see the cathedrals they worked on completed."],
g:[["Flying buttress","An outside support carrying a wall’s push"],["Ribbed vault","A ceiling supported by crossing arches"],["Rose window","A round stained-glass window"],["Gargoyle","A carved waterspout"]],
f:["Cologne Cathedral took over 600 years to complete, finished in 1880.","Gargoyles carry rainwater away from walls.","Chartres Cathedral keeps much of its original medieval stained glass."]},
jazz:{s:[
["Congo Square","In New Orleans, enslaved Africans gathered on Sundays in Congo Square to drum, sing and dance, keeping African musical traditions alive. These rhythms, along with blues, spirituals, brass bands, ragtime and Caribbean music, all flowed into jazz around 1900. New Orleans is called the birthplace of jazz."],
["Improvisation and swing","Jazz is known for improvisation, making up music on the spot, and swing, a lilting, bouncing rhythm. Musicians often take turns playing solos over a repeated chord pattern. Syncopation, stressing off-beats, gives jazz its energy. Every performance of a jazz tune can be different."],
["Louis Armstrong","Louis Armstrong, born in New Orleans in 1901, became one of the most influential musicians of the 20th century. His brilliant trumpet solos in the 1920s made the soloist the star of jazz. His gravelly voice made songs such as What a Wonderful World famous. He also popularized scat singing, using nonsense syllables."],
["Duke Ellington and the big bands","In the 1930s and 1940s, big bands with sections of trumpets, trombones and saxophones played swing music for dancers. Duke Ellington, a composer and bandleader, wrote more than a thousand pieces. Count Basie, Benny Goodman and Glenn Miller led popular bands, and singers such as Ella Fitzgerald and Billie Holiday became stars."],
["Bebop and beyond","In the 1940s, musicians such as Charlie Parker and Dizzy Gillespie created bebop, fast, complex jazz for listening rather than dancing. Later styles included cool jazz, Miles Davis’s Kind of Blue of 1959, the best-selling jazz album; hard bop; John Coltrane’s spiritual jazz; free jazz; and jazz fusion with rock and funk."],
["Women in jazz","Women have shaped jazz from the start. Billie Holiday sang with heartbreaking feeling, and Ella Fitzgerald, the First Lady of Song, was famous for her scat singing. Mary Lou Williams composed and arranged for the great bands, and Sarah Vaughan and Nina Simone became legends. Today musicians such as Esperanza Spalding carry jazz forward."]],
sp:["International Jazz Day","In 2011 UNESCO declared 30 April International Jazz Day, at the suggestion of pianist Herbie Hancock, to celebrate jazz as a music of freedom, creativity and dialogue between cultures. Each year a global concert is held in a different host city, featuring musicians from around the world, and events are held in more than 190 countries. Jazz is often called America’s classical music."],
g:[["Improvisation","Making up music while playing"],["Swing","A lilting jazz rhythm"],["Syncopation","Stressing off-beats"],["Scat","Singing with nonsense syllables"]],
f:["The saxophone was invented by Adolphe Sax in the 1840s.","The first jazz recording was made in 1917.","Ella Fitzgerald was called the First Lady of Song."]},
austen:{s:[
["Steventon","Jane Austen was born on 16 December 1775 in Steventon, Hampshire, England, the seventh of eight children of a clergyman. Her family loved reading and performed plays at home. As a teenager she wrote funny stories and parodies, now called her Juvenilia, to entertain her family."],
["Bath and Chawton","In 1801 the family moved to the fashionable city of Bath, which Austen disliked. After her father died in 1805, she, her mother and sister moved several times. In 1809 her brother Edward gave them a cottage in Chawton, Hampshire, where she revised and wrote her novels. It is now Jane Austen’s House museum."],
["Published anonymously","Austen’s novels were published anonymously, as was common for women writers. Sense and Sensibility, 1811, was credited to A Lady. Pride and Prejudice, 1813, followed. Only after her death did her brother Henry reveal her name to the public. She earned only modest money from her books in her lifetime."],
["Elizabeth and Darcy","Pride and Prejudice follows Elizabeth Bennet, a witty young woman, and the proud, wealthy Mr. Darcy. Each misjudges the other, and they must overcome their pride and prejudice to fall in love. Its first line is famous: It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife."],
["Money and marriage","Austen’s novels show the lives of the English gentry, where women could rarely inherit property or earn money, so marriage decided their future. With irony and humor, she examined money, class, manners and the importance of marrying for love rather than wealth alone."],
["Death and legacy","Austen fell ill in 1816 and died on 18 July 1817 in Winchester, aged 41. She is buried in Winchester Cathedral. Persuasion and Northanger Abbey were published after her death. Her novels are now among the most loved in English and have inspired countless films, TV series and modern retellings."]],
sp:["Austen on screen","Austen’s novels have been adapted again and again. The 1995 BBC series of Pride and Prejudice, starring Colin Firth, drew millions of viewers. Clueless, 1995, reset Emma in a Beverly Hills high school, and Bridget Jones’s Diary drew on Pride and Prejudice. Bollywood made Bride and Prejudice in 2004. In 2017, for the 200th anniversary of her death, her portrait appeared on Britain’s ten-pound note."],
g:[["Irony","Saying the opposite of what is meant, often humorously"],["Gentry","Wealthy landowners below the nobility"],["Anonymous","Without the author’s name"],["Heroine","The main female character"]],
f:["Austen completed six novels.","Her sister Cassandra burned many of Jane’s letters.","Janeites are fans of Jane Austen."]}
});

